`arr.includes(x)` scans the array element by element (O(n) per lookup), while `set.has(x)` uses hashing and is roughly constant time. Doing many lookups against a large array multiplies that cost; building a `Set` once removes it.

```js
const bannedIds = [3, 7, 9];
const bannedSet = new Set(bannedIds);

const users = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 7 }, { id: 8 }];
const allowed = users.filter(u => !bannedSet.has(u.id));
allowed; // [{ id: 1 }, { id: 8 }]
```

For small arrays the difference is negligible, so choose what reads best. A `Set` also guarantees uniqueness, which is a bonus when values must not repeat.

