back:
`fill(value)` overwrites every slot (or a start/end range) with `value`, **mutating** the array and returning it. It stores the *same* value in each slot, so with an object or array, every slot points to **one shared object**.

```js
const grid = Array(3).fill([]);
grid[0].push('x');
grid; // [['x'], ['x'], ['x']]

const ok = Array.from({ length: 3 }, () => []);
ok[0].push('x');
ok; // [['x'], [], []]
```

Use `fill()` for primitives (`0`, `null`, `''`). For objects or nested arrays, use `Array.from` with a function so each slot gets a new one.

