back:
Modern runtimes (Node 22+, current browsers) have it built in: `a.intersection(b)` returns a new `Set`. Without it, keep the values of `a` that `b` also has: `new Set([...a].filter(x => b.has(x)))`.

```js
const a = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const b = new Set([4, 3, 2]);

a.intersection(b); // Set(2) { 2, 3 }
new Set([...a].filter(x => b.has(x))); // Set(2) { 2, 3 }
```

`b.has()` is a fast hash lookup, so the manual version is linear in the size of `a`. For arrays, wrap them: `new Set(arr1).intersection(new Set(arr2))`.

