Spread both into a new `Set`: `new Set([...a, ...b])`. A `Set` keeps only one copy of each value. Node 22+ and current browsers also have `a.union(b)`.

```js
const a = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const b = new Set([4, 3, 2]);
new Set([...a, ...b]); // Set(4) { 1, 2, 3, 4 }
a.union(b); // Set(4) { 1, 2, 3, 4 }

const tags = [...new Set(['js', 'css', 'js'])];
tags; // ['js', 'css']
```

Uniqueness uses SameValueZero (like `===`), so two different objects with the same content are both kept.

