It is the values that are in **exactly one** of the two sets (in either, but not both). Compute `a - b` and `b - a` and combine them; Node 22+ and current browsers have `a.symmetricDifference(b)`.

```js
const a = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const b = new Set([4, 3, 2]);

a.symmetricDifference(b); // Set(2) { 1, 4 }
new Set([
  ...[...a].filter(x => !b.has(x)),
  ...[...b].filter(x => !a.has(x)),
]); // Set(2) { 1, 4 }
```

Example use: comparing two permission lists to see what changed in either direction.

