Combining `async` with a `for` (or a `for...of`) loop is possibly the most straightforward option when performing asynchronous operations over array elements. Using `await` inside a `for` loop will cause the code to **stop and wait** for the asynchronous operation to complete before continuing. This means that **all promises will be run sequentially**.

```js
const asyncUppercase = item =>
  new Promise(resolve =>
    setTimeout(
      () => resolve(item.toUpperCase()),
      Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000)
    )
  );

const uppercaseItems = async () => {
  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
  for (const item of items) {
    const uppercaseItem = await asyncUppercase(item);
    console.log(uppercaseItem);
  }

  console.log('Items processed');
};

uppercaseItems();
// Logs: 'A', 'B', 'C', 'Items processed'
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/async-array-loops.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
**One after another.** Each `await` pauses the loop until that promise settles, so the calls run sequentially, in order. To run them in parallel, start them all with `map()` and wait for all of them with `Promise.all()`.

```js
const saveAll = async (items, save) => {
  for (const item of items) {
    await save(item); // next save starts only after this one finishes
  }
};

const saveAllParallel = (items, save) => Promise.all(items.map(item => save(item)));
```

Sequential is right when order matters or an API rate-limits you; parallel is faster for independent requests. Do not use `forEach(async ...)`: `forEach()` ignores the returned promises, so nothing waits for them.

---
