Start `reduce()` with `[]` and only `push` the transformed value when the item passes your test. `flatMap()` does the same more readably: return `[value]` to keep an item or `[]` to drop it.

```js
const files = ['foo.txt ', '.bar', '   ', 'baz.foo'];

const viaReduce = files.reduce((acc, file) => {
  const name = file.trim();
  if (name) acc.push(`~/app/${name}`);
  return acc;
}, []);

const viaFlatMap = files.flatMap(file => {
  const name = file.trim();
  return name ? [`~/app/${name}`] : [];
});

viaReduce; // ['~/app/foo.txt', '~/app/.bar', '~/app/baz.foo']
viaFlatMap; // ['~/app/foo.txt', '~/app/.bar', '~/app/baz.foo']
```

Both visit each element once. Many teams still prefer a `map`/`filter` chain for clarity unless the array is large.

