Have you ever needed to combine two arrays of objects? Maybe each one contains partial information about the same objects, and you want to merge them into a single array. So how would you go about doing that?

Supposing you know the **key that uniquely identifies each object**, you can use `Object.values()` and the spread operator (`...`) to get all the objects in both arrays. Then, you can use `Array.prototype.reduce()` to **combine** them based on the specified key.

For each value in the arrays, you can check if the object has the specified key. If it does, you can add it to the accumulator object, combining it with any existing object that has the same key. This will result in a single object for each unique value of the key property.

```js
const combine = (a, b, prop) =>
  Object.values(
    [...a, ...b].reduce((acc, v) => {
      if (v[prop])
        acc[v[prop]] = acc[v[prop]]
          ? { ...acc[v[prop]], ...v }
          : { ...v };
      return acc;
    }, {})
  );

const x = [
  { id: 1, name: 'John' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Maria' }
];
const y = [
  { id: 1, age: 28 },
  { id: 3, age: 26 },
  { age: 3}
];
combine(x, y, 'id');
// [
//  { id: 1, name: 'John', age: 28 },
//  { id: 2, name: 'Maria' },
//  { id: 3, age: 26 }
// ]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/combine-object-arrays.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Index everything by the key in a `Map`: for each object, merge it into whatever is already stored for its key with object spread, then return the map's values. Objects without the key are skipped.

```js
const combine = (a, b, key) => {
  const byKey = new Map();
  for (const item of [...a, ...b]) {
    const k = item[key];
    if (k == null) continue;
    byKey.set(k, { ...byKey.get(k), ...item });
  }
  return [...byKey.values()];
};

const users = [{ id: 0, name: 'Ana' }, { id: 2, name: 'Ben' }];
const ages = [{ id: 0, age: 28 }, { id: 3, age: 26 }, { age: 3 }];
combine(users, ages, 'id');
// [{ id: 0, name: 'Ana', age: 28 }, { id: 2, name: 'Ben' }, { id: 3, age: 26 }]
```

Pitfall: a truthiness check like `if (item[key])` drops a record whose id is `0`. Later arrays win on conflicting fields because their spread comes last.

