Serialize each row by joining its values with `,`, then join the rows with `\n`. Any value that contains a comma, a double quote or a newline must be wrapped in double quotes, with inner quotes doubled (`"` → `""`).

```js
const serializeValue = v => {
  if (v == null) return '';
  const s = String(v);
  return /[",\n]/.test(s) ? `"${s.replaceAll('"', '""')}"` : s;
};
const arrayToCSV = rows =>
  rows.map(row => row.map(serializeValue).join(',')).join('\n');

arrayToCSV([['a', 'b'], ['c', 'd']]); // 'a,b\nc,d'
arrayToCSV([['a,b', 'say "hi"']]); // '"a,b","say ""hi"""'
```

A plain `row.join(',')` breaks as soon as a value (like an address) contains a comma. For user-facing exports, a library such as Papa Parse also handles encodings and Excel quirks.

