Use `structuredClone()` when you need a **deep copy**: nested objects and arrays are copied too, so changing the copy never touches the original. It also handles `Date`, `Map`, `Set` and circular references.

```js
const rows = [{ id: 1, tags: ['a'] }];
const copy = structuredClone(rows);
copy[0].tags.push('b');
rows[0].tags; // ['a']  (untouched)
```

It **cannot** clone functions (throws `DataCloneError`), DOM nodes, or class behavior: a class instance comes back as a plain object without its methods. `[...arr]` is only shallow, so the objects inside are still shared.

