JavaScript's `Symbol.iterator` is a very powerful tool that every web developer should learn how to use. It allows you to define and customize the way a value is iterated, effectively allowing you to make any value iterable. You can easily apply this knowledge to plain JavaScript objects and even classes.

[Quick refresher](https://www.30secondsofcode.org/js/s/iterators)

All you need to correctly define an iterator is a generator function `yield`ing each of the iteration values. This could be used to retrieve key-value pairs in an object, call specific getter functions from a class or split a number into an array of digits:

```js
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 };

obj[Symbol.iterator] = function* () {
  for (let key of Object.keys(obj)) yield { [key]: obj[key] };
};

[...obj]; // [ { a: 1 }, { b: 2 }, { c: 3 }]

class IterableNumber extends Number {
  *[Symbol.iterator]() {
    for (let digit of [...`${this}`].map(d => Number.parseInt(d))) yield digit;
  }
}

const num = new IterableNumber(1337);
[...num]; // [ 1, 3, 3, 7]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/make-iterable.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Give it a `[Symbol.iterator]` method that returns an iterator. The easiest way is a generator method, `*[Symbol.iterator]() { ... }`, which `yield`s each value.

```js
class TodoList {
  #items = [];
  add(text) { this.#items.push(text); return this; }
  *[Symbol.iterator]() {
    yield* this.#items; // delegate to the array's own iterator
  }
}

const list = new TodoList().add('Write tests').add('Ship');
[...list]; // ['Write tests', 'Ship']
for (const t of list) console.log(t);

// Works on a plain object too:
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
obj[Symbol.iterator] = function* () {
  for (const key of Object.keys(this)) yield [key, this[key]];
};
[...obj]; // [['a', 1], ['b', 2]]
```

Plain objects are not iterable by default: `for (const x of {})` throws a `TypeError`. For one-off loops over an object, `Object.entries(obj)` is usually simpler than adding an iterator.

