The later source wins for a duplicate key, and the merge is shallow: a nested object from the later source replaces the earlier one entirely instead of being merged into it.

```js
const defaults = { theme: 'light', pageSize: 20, notify: { email: true, sms: false } };
const saved = { pageSize: 50, notify: { sms: true } };

const prefs = { ...defaults, ...saved };
// { theme: 'light', pageSize: 50, notify: { sms: true } }  <- notify.email is gone

Object.assign({}, saved, defaults).pageSize; // 20 (defaults came last, so they win)
```

`Object.assign(target, ...sources)` mutates and returns `target`, so pass `{}` first unless you really mean to modify an existing object. For nested settings, merge that level explicitly: `notify: { ...defaults.notify, ...saved.notify }`.

