back:
Find the mean, square each value's distance from it, average those squares (the variance), and take the square root. The population version divides by `n`. The sample version divides by `n - 1` because it estimates the spread of a larger group from a subset.

```js
const standardDeviation = (arr, usePopulation = false) => {
  const mean = arr.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0) / arr.length;
  const squaredDiffs = arr.map(val => (val - mean) ** 2);
  const divisor = arr.length - (usePopulation ? 0 : 1);
  return Math.sqrt(squaredDiffs.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0) / divisor);
};

standardDeviation([10, 2, 38, 23, 38, 23, 21]); // 13.284434142114991 (sample)
standardDeviation([10, 2, 38, 23, 38, 23, 21], true); // 12.29899614287479 (population)
```

Use the population version when you have every value (e.g. all response times in a log), and the sample version when you have a sample. A single-element array gives `NaN` for the sample version (it divides by 0).

