The weighted average of an array is the result of **multiplying each number in the array by a corresponding weight**, adding the results together and then **dividing the resulting sum by the sum of the weights**.

You can use `Array.prototype.reduce()` to multiply each value with the corresponding weight and add the results together, then divide the resulting sum by the sum of the weights.

```js
const weightedAverage = (nums, weights) => {
  const [sum, weightSum] = weights.reduce(
    (acc, w, i) => {
      acc[0] = acc[0] + nums[i] * w;
      acc[1] = acc[1] + w;
      return acc;
    },
    [0, 0]
  );
  return sum / weightSum;
};

weightedAverage([1, 2, 3], [0.6, 0.2, 0.3]); // 1.72727
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/numeric-array-math-operations.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Add up `value × weight` for every item, then divide by the sum of the weights. A single `reduce()` can track both totals at once.

```js
const weightedAverage = (nums, weights) => {
  const [sum, weightSum] = weights.reduce(
    ([s, ws], w, i) => [s + nums[i] * w, ws + w],
    [0, 0]
  );
  return sum / weightSum;
};

weightedAverage([90, 80, 70], [0.5, 0.3, 0.2]); // 83
weightedAverage([1, 2, 3], [0.6, 0.2, 0.3]); // 1.727272727272727 (19/11)
```

Dividing by the weight sum means the weights don't have to add up to 1. If they add up to 0, the result is `NaN`.

