The median of an array is the **middle number when the array is sorted**. If the array has an **odd number of elements**, the median is the middle number. If the array has an **even number of elements**, the median is the average of the two middle numbers.

You can use `Array.prototype.sort()` to sort the values, then return the number at the midpoint if `Array.prototype.length` is odd, otherwise the average of the two middle numbers.

```js
const median = arr => {
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2),
    nums = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  return arr.length % 2 !== 0 ? nums[mid] : (nums[mid - 1] + nums[mid]) / 2;
};

median([5, 6, 50, 1, -5]); // 5
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/numeric-array-math-operations.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Sort a copy numerically, then take the middle element (odd length) or the average of the two middle elements (even length). Without `(a, b) => a - b`, `sort()` compares values as strings, so `[10, 9, 1]` sorts to `[1, 10, 9]`.

```js
const median = arr => {
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const nums = arr.toSorted((a, b) => a - b); // copy, then sort
  return arr.length % 2 !== 0 ? nums[mid] : (nums[mid - 1] + nums[mid]) / 2;
};

median([5, 6, 50, 1, -5]); // 5
median([10, 9, 1, 2]); // 5.5
[10, 9, 1].sort(); // [1, 10, 9]
```

`toSorted()` (ES2023) returns a sorted copy. `[...arr].sort(...)` does the same in older code. Plain `arr.sort()` would reorder the caller's array.

