Wrap the array in a `Proxy` whose `get` trap turns a negative numeric key into `length + index` before reading the property. In real code, use `arr.at(-1)` instead. It is built in, works in all modern browsers and Node, and needs no wrapper.

```js
const handler = {
  get(target, key, receiver) {
    // keys arrive as strings ('-1'); symbol keys (used by spread) can't go through Number()
    const index = typeof key === 'string' ? Number(key) : NaN;
    const prop = index < 0 ? `${target.length + index}` : key;
    return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
  },
};

const arr = new Proxy(['a', 'b', 'c'], handler);
arr[-1]; // 'c'
arr[-2]; // 'b'
arr.length; // 3 (Number('length') is NaN, so it passes through)
[...arr]; // ['a', 'b', 'c']

['a', 'b', 'c'].at(-2); // 'b'
```

A Proxy adds overhead to every property access and surprises other developers, so treat this as a way to learn how `Proxy` traps work, not a pattern to ship.

