Each `gen.next()` call runs the generator up to its next `yield` and returns `{ value, done }`. Keep calling until `done` is `true`. At that point `value` is `undefined`, or whatever the generator `return`ed.

```js
const range = function* (end) {
  for (let i = 0; i < end; i++) yield i;
};

const gen = range(3);
gen.next(); // { value: 0, done: false }
gen.next(); // { value: 1, done: false }
gen.next(); // { value: 2, done: false }
gen.next(); // { value: undefined, done: true }
```

Pulling values by hand is useful when you want the next item only on demand, e.g. the next page of results when the user clicks "Load more". A finished generator stays finished: more `next()` calls keep returning `done: true`.

