Declare the parameters as `(start, end, step = 1)`. If `end` is `undefined`, only one argument was passed, so shift it: `end` becomes `start` and `start` becomes `0`.

```js
const range = function* (start, end, step = 1) {
  if (end === undefined) [start, end] = [0, start];
  for (let i = start; i < end; i += step) yield i;
};

[...range(4)]; // [0, 1, 2, 3]
[...range(6, 10)]; // [6, 7, 8, 9]
[...range(2, 10, 2)]; // [2, 4, 6, 8]
[...range(10, 0, -1)]; // [] (the loop only counts up)
```

Pitfalls: `step = 0` loops forever, and a negative step yields nothing because the condition is `i < end`. Validate `step` (throw if it's `<= 0`) or add a counting-down branch.

