back:
Loop from the last index down to `0` and `splice(i, 1)` each match, collecting what you remove. Going backwards matters: removing an item shifts all later items down one index, so a forward loop would skip the item right after each removal.

```js
const spliceFilter = (arr, fn) => {
  const removed = [];
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (fn(arr[i])) removed.unshift(...arr.splice(i, 1));
  }
  return removed;
};

const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6];
spliceFilter(nums, n => n % 2 === 0); // [2, 4, 6]
nums;                                 // [1, 3]

// Forward-loop bug: the 4 is skipped
const fw = [2, 4, 5];
for (let i = 0; i < fw.length; i++) if (fw[i] % 2 === 0) fw.splice(i, 1);
fw; // [4, 5]
```

Usually you don't need mutation at all: `arr.filter(x => !fn(x))` is simpler. Mutate only when other code holds a reference to the same array.

