back:
Find its position with `findIndex()`. If it is `-1`, return `[...arr, val]`; otherwise return `arr.with(i, val)` (ES2023), which copies the array with one slot replaced. This is the usual "upsert" for a list in React state after a save request returns the updated record.

```js
const replaceOrAppend = (arr, val, compFn) => {
  const i = arr.findIndex(v => compFn(v, val));
  return i === -1 ? [...arr, val] : arr.with(i, val);
};

const people = [{ id: 1, name: 'John' }, { id: 2, name: 'Jane' }];
const byId = (a, b) => a.id === b.id;

replaceOrAppend(people, { id: 2, name: 'Janet' }, byId);
// [{ id: 1, name: 'John' }, { id: 2, name: 'Janet' }]
replaceOrAppend(people, { id: 3, name: 'Jack' }, byId);
// [{ id: 1, ... }, { id: 2, ... }, { id: 3, name: 'Jack' }]
people.length; // 2 (original unchanged)
```

Without `with()`, copy first and then mutate the copy: `const res = [...arr]; res.splice(i, 1, val);`. Either way the copy is shallow: the untouched objects are shared with the original, so don't edit them in place.

