Write one compare function that checks the properties in order and returns the first non-zero result, so a later property only breaks ties in the earlier ones. For each descending property, flip the sign of that comparison.

```js
const orderBy = (arr, props, orders = []) =>
  arr.toSorted((a, b) => {
    for (const [i, prop] of props.entries()) {
      const dir = orders[i] === 'desc' ? -1 : 1;
      if (a[prop] < b[prop]) return -dir;
      if (a[prop] > b[prop]) return dir;
    }
    return 0; // equal on every property
  });

const users = [
  { name: 'fred', age: 48 },
  { name: 'barney', age: 36 },
  { name: 'fred', age: 40 },
];

orderBy(users, ['name', 'age'], ['asc', 'desc']);
// barney 36, fred 48, fred 40
orderBy(users, ['name', 'age']);
// barney 36, fred 40, fred 48
```

`<` compares strings by character code (`'Z' < 'a'`); for human-facing text use `localeCompare()` for string properties. `toSorted()` leaves `users` unchanged, and since sorting is stable, ties keep their original order.

