Any iterable can be chunked in a similar fashion, using a **generator function**. The only difference is that the iterable needs to be iterated over using a `for...of` loop, instead of `Array.prototype.slice()`. This is due to the fact that the **number of chunks cannot be determined in advance**.

Each value iterated is added to the current `chunk`, using `Array.prototype.push()`. Once the `chunk` reaches the specified `size`, you can `yield` the value and reset the `chunk` to an empty array. Finally, you can check if the final `chunk` is non-empty and `yield` it as well.

```js
const chunkify = function* (itr, size) {
  let chunk = [];
  for (const v of itr) {
    chunk.push(v);
    if (chunk.length === size) {
      yield chunk;
      chunk = [];
    }
  }
  if (chunk.length) yield chunk;
};

const x = new Set([1, 2, 1, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5]);

[...chunkify(x, 2)]; // [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/split-array-into-chunks.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use a generator: walk the iterable with `for...of`, push each value into the current `chunk`, and `yield` it whenever it reaches `size`, starting a new one; after the loop, yield the leftover partial chunk. `slice()` doesn't work here because a general iterable has no length or indexes.

```js
function* chunkify(itr, size) {
  let chunk = [];
  for (const v of itr) {
    chunk.push(v);
    if (chunk.length === size) {
      yield chunk;
      chunk = [];
    }
  }
  if (chunk.length) yield chunk; // don't lose the last partial chunk
}

const ids = new Set([1, 2, 1, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5]); // {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
[...chunkify(ids, 2)]; // [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]
```

The generator is lazy: each chunk is built only when the consumer asks for the next one, e.g. `for (const batch of chunkify(rows, 100)) await insert(batch);`.

