back:
It creates a new array holding the same elements: a **shallow** copy. Primitives are copied, but objects inside are shared by both arrays, so editing `copy[0].name` also changes the original. For an array of objects, update items immutably or deep-copy with `structuredClone()`.

```js
const users = [{ id: 1, name: 'Ann' }];

const copy = [...users];
copy === users;        // false (new array)
copy[0].name = 'Bob';
users[0].name;         // 'Bob' (same object!)

// Immutable update of one item:
const renamed = users.map(u => (u.id === 1 ? { ...u, name: 'Cy' } : u));

// Full deep copy:
const deep = structuredClone(users);
```

For arrays of numbers or strings (tags, ids), `[...arr]` is all you need.

