back:
Rest syntax gathers the *remaining* items into a new array (or object): leftover function arguments, leftover array elements in destructuring, or leftover properties in object destructuring. It must come last. Spread looks the same but does the opposite: it expands values out.

```js
// Function parameters
const fn = (str, ...nums) => `${str}_${nums.join('')}`;
fn('hi', 1, 2, 3); // 'hi_123'  (nums is [1, 2, 3])

// Array destructuring
const [head, ...tail] = [1, 2, 3]; // head 1, tail [2, 3]

// Object destructuring: strip a field before sending a user to the client
const { password, ...safeUser } = { id: 1, email: 'a@b.c', password: 'x' };
safeUser; // { id: 1, email: 'a@b.c' }
```

`(...a, b) => {}` is a `SyntaxError`: a rest element must be the last one. Rest parameters give a real array, unlike the old `arguments` object.

