back:
Find the first item that **fails** with `findIndex(x => !fn(x))` and return `arr.slice(0, index)`. If nothing fails, `findIndex` returns `-1` and every item qualifies, so return a copy of the whole array.

```js
const takeWhile = (arr, fn) => {
  const index = arr.findIndex(x => !fn(x));
  return index === -1 ? arr.slice() : arr.slice(0, index);
};

takeWhile([1, 2, 3, 4], n => n < 0); // []
takeWhile([1, 2, 3, 4], n => n < 3); // [1, 2]
takeWhile([1, 2, 3, 4], n => n < 5); // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

Unlike `filter()`, this stops at the first failure: `takeWhile([1, 5, 2], n => n < 3)` is `[1]`, not `[1, 2]`. Useful on sorted data, e.g. taking orders from a newest-first list while they are from today. Without the `-1` check, `slice(0, -1)` would drop the last item.

