Make as many rows as the longest array has items, and build row `i` by taking index `i` from every array: `arrays.map(a => a[i])`. Shorter arrays contribute `undefined` for positions they don't have.

```js
const zip = (...arrays) => {
  const maxLength = Math.max(...arrays.map(a => a.length));
  return Array.from({ length: maxLength }, (_, i) => arrays.map(a => a[i]));
};

zip(['a', 'b'], [1, 2], [true, false]); // [['a', 1, true], ['b', 2, false]]
zip(['a'], [1, 2], [true, false]);      // [['a', 1, true], [undefined, 2, false]]
```

Useful when an API returns parallel arrays, e.g. `labels` and `values`, and you want one `[label, value]` pair per row. For equal-length arrays, zip and unzip undo each other (both are a transpose); with uneven lengths the `undefined` padding makes the round trip differ.

