A stored attribute holds data copied from the source row (a database column or JSON field). A computed attribute is derived from stored ones on each read; in JavaScript it is usually a `get` accessor, so callers read it like a field, without parentheses.

```js
class Author {
  constructor({ id, name, surname }) {
    Object.assign(this, { id, name, surname }); // stored
  }
  get fullName() {                               // computed
    return `${this.name} ${this.surname}`;
  }
}

const a = new Author({ id: 1, name: 'Ada', surname: 'Lovelace' });
a.fullName;         // 'Ada Lovelace'
JSON.stringify(a);  // '{"id":1,"name":"Ada","surname":"Lovelace"}'
```

Pitfall: getters live on the prototype, so `{ ...a }` and `JSON.stringify(a)` leave them out. Add them explicitly when building an API response.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/complex-object-attributes-relationships.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Store the foreign key on the "many" side and define getters that look up the related records: a single relationship returns one record, a multiple relationship returns an array.

```js
class Post extends Model {
  get author() {             // single: one Author
    return Author.find(this.authorId);
  }
}

class Author extends Model {
  get posts() {              // multiple: array of Posts
    return Post.all.filter(post => post.authorId === this.id);
  }
}

post.author.name;   // 'Ada'
author.posts.length; // 2
```

This covers one-to-one and one-to-many. Many-to-many (posts and tags) needs a join model such as `PostTag { postId, tagId }`, exactly like a join table in SQL. Each getter call re-runs the lookup, so index by id (a `Map`) if the collections are large.

