back:
Looking a record up by id is the most common query. `array.find()` scans every record (O(n)); `map.get(id)` is a hash lookup (O(1) on average). Keep the array for ordered iteration and the `Map` for id lookups, and update both whenever a record is added.

```js
class Model {
  static instances = {};        // { Post: [records] }
  static indexedInstances = {}; // { Post: Map(id => record) }

  static prepare(model) {
    Model.instances[model.name] ??= [];
    Model.indexedInstances[model.name] ??= new Map();
  }

  constructor(data) {
    Object.assign(this, data);
    const name = this.constructor.name;
    Model.instances[name].push(this);
    Model.indexedInstances[name].set(data.id, this);
  }

  static find(id) {
    return Model.indexedInstances[this.name].get(id);
  }
}
```

Pitfall: `Map` keys are compared by type, so `Post.find('1')` misses a record stored under `1`. Ids from URLs or form fields are strings; convert with `Number(id)` first.

