Because `record[key]` runs a getter exactly as it reads a stored property, so any computed attribute or relationship can appear in a query. The helper doesn't need to know which is which.

```js
class Post extends Model {
  get isPublished() { return this.publishedAt !== null; }
}
class Author extends Model {
  get postCount() { return this.posts.length; }
}

Post.where({ isPublished: true });           // posts with a publishedAt date
Author.where({ postCount: n => n > 1 });     // authors with 2+ posts
```

Trade-off: the getter runs for every record on every query, and there is no index behind it. That's fine for hundreds of records in memory; for large data, filter in the database instead.

