Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/complex-object-autoloading-console.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

1) A script that starts `node:repl` and preloads your modules into `server.context`, so classes are available immediately. 2) A custom inspect method, keyed by `Symbol.for('nodejs.util.inspect.custom')`, so records print as short readable summaries instead of full object dumps.

```js
class Post {
  constructor(id, title) { this.id = id; this.title = title; }

  [Symbol.for('nodejs.util.inspect.custom')]() {
    return `Post #${this.id} "${this.title}"`;
  }
}

console.log(new Post(1, 'Hello'));   // Post #1 "Hello"
console.log([new Post(1, 'Hello')]); // [ Post #1 "Hello" ]
```

The custom inspect hook affects `console.log` and the REPL in Node only; browsers ignore it, and `JSON.stringify` is unaffected.

