Subclassing `Array` gives you `filter`, `find`, `map`, `reduce`, `sort` and `for...of` for free, so you only add query helpers such as `where` or `pluck`. Methods like `filter()` and `map()` called on a subclass instance return an instance of that subclass, so query results can be chained.

```js
class RecordSet extends Array {
  get first() { return this[0]; }
}

const posts = RecordSet.from([{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }, { id: 3 }]);
const recent = posts.filter(p => p.id > 1);
recent instanceof RecordSet; // true
recent.first;                // { id: 2 }
```

Pitfall: `new RecordSet(3)` makes an empty array of length 3, just like `new Array(3)`. Build instances with `RecordSet.from(array)` or `RecordSet.of(...items)`.

