back:
For each key in the query, check the type of the expected value: a function is called with the record's value, an array means "one of these", and anything else is compared with `===`. A record matches only if every key matches (`every`), so keys are combined with AND.

```js
class RecordSet extends Array {
  where(query) {
    return this.filter(record =>
      Object.keys(query).every(key => {
        const expected = query[key];
        if (typeof expected === 'function') return expected(record[key]);
        if (Array.isArray(expected)) return expected.includes(record[key]);
        return record[key] === expected;
      })
    );
  }
}

posts.where({ status: ['draft', 'published'], views: v => v > 10 });
```

Pitfalls: because arrays mean "one of", you can't match an array-valued field by equality; and `===` compares objects and `Date`s by reference, so use a function for those.

