`this` is the class the method was called on, here `Post`, not the base class where the method was written. That lets one static method in `Model` serve every subclass by using `this.name` to find the right records.

```js
class Model {
  static registry = {};
  static get all() {
    return Model.registry[this.name] ?? [];
  }
}

class Post extends Model {
  static { Model.registry[this.name] = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }]; }
}

Post.all.length; // 2  (this === Post, this.name === 'Post')
```

