back:
It lets you load a read-only snapshot of related plain objects (for example a JSON export) into memory, wrap them in model classes, and query and follow relationships (`Post.where(...)`, `post.author`) without a database.

```js
// data.json -> models -> queries
posts.forEach(row => new Post(row));
Post.where({ authorId: 1 }).pluck('title');
```

It is the wrong tool when the data changes, is shared between processes or users, or is too big for memory. Those cases need a real database and an ORM or query builder. The idea borrows ActiveRecord's API style (from Ruby on Rails), not its persistence.

