back:
Loop over the arguments and branch on their type: a string is a trait name, a function is called with the data built so far and its result is merged, and anything else is merged as an object. Order matters, because each step sees the previous ones.

```js
static build(...args) {
  const { model, base, traits } = Factory.factoryMap.get(this.name);
  const data = { ...base };
  for (const arg of args) {
    if (typeof arg === 'string') Object.assign(data, traits[arg]);
    else if (typeof arg === 'function') Object.assign(data, arg(data));
    else Object.assign(data, arg); // plain object override
  }
  return new model(data);
}

PostFactory.build('unpublished', { title: 'Custom title' });
// Post { id: 1, title: 'Custom title', publishedAt: null }

AuthorFactory.build(
  { name: 'John' },
  data => ({ email: `${data.name.toLowerCase()}@authornet.io` })
);
// Author { ..., name: 'John', email: 'john@authornet.io' }
```

A function argument can return several derived fields at once, which keeps tests readable without defining a trait for every special case.

