Fixtures are fixed, hand-written data files or objects that tests share. Factories are functions that build fresh test objects from sensible defaults plus per-test overrides. Factories age better because a model change is updated in one place, and each test states only the fields it cares about.

```js
// Fixture: every test gets the same frozen snapshot
import post from './fixtures/post.json';

// Factory: defaults + just what this test needs
const draft = PostFactory.build({ publishedAt: null });
```

Shared fixtures drift: someone edits `post.json` for one test and unrelated tests start failing. The JS pattern here is modelled on Ruby's factory_bot; libraries such as `fishery` offer the same idea for JavaScript and TypeScript.

