Keep a `Map` (or `Set`) of the values already used for that field. On create, normalize the value, throw if it's already present, otherwise store it. The same `Map` doubles as an index for fast lookup by that field.

```js
class User {
  static byEmail = new Map();

  constructor(data) {
    const key = data.email.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (User.byEmail.has(key)) {
      throw new Error(`Duplicate email: ${data.email}`);
    }
    Object.assign(this, data);
    User.byEmail.set(key, this);
  }
}

new User({ email: 'a@x.io' });
new User({ email: 'A@x.io' }); // Error: Duplicate email: A@x.io
```

Normalize before comparing, or `A@x.io` and `a@x.io` both get in. In a real app, also add a `UNIQUE` constraint in the database: two requests can pass an application check at the same moment.

