Make every scope return the same collection type it was called on. The next scope then filters the previous result. With an `Array` subclass this is automatic, because `filter()` on a subclass returns that subclass.

```js
class PostSet extends Array {
  get published() {
    return this.filter(p => p.publishedAt !== null);
  }
  byAuthor(id) {
    return this.filter(p => p.authorId === id);
  }
}

const posts = PostSet.from(rows);
posts.published.byAuthor(1); // PostSet of author 1's published posts
```

Each scope is a named rule, so `posts.published.byAuthor(1)` reads like the requirement. Chaining in memory filters one step at a time; a database ORM instead combines the scopes into one SQL `WHERE`.

