Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/complex-object-scopes.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

A scope is a named, reusable query defined on the model, such as `published` or `recent`. Defining it once keeps the rule in one place (DRY: Don't Repeat Yourself), gives it a readable name, and means a change to the rule is made in one spot.

```js
class Post extends Model {
  static get published() {
    return this.where({ status: 'published' });
  }
}

Post.published; // instead of Post.where({ status: 'published' }) everywhere
```

In Rails this is `scope :published, -> { where(status: 'published') }`. Scopes are most useful when they can be chained, so each should return a queryable collection rather than a plain value.

