`JSON.stringify` only outputs an object's own enumerable properties, and class getters live on the prototype, so they are skipped. A serializer lists the attribute names to output and reads each one with `post[name]`, which runs the getter.

```js
class Post {
  constructor(title) { this.title = title; }
  get excerpt() { return this.title.slice(0, 3); }
}

class PostSerializer {
  static attributes = ['title', 'excerpt'];
  static serialize(post) {
    return Object.fromEntries(this.attributes.map(a => [a, post[a]]));
  }
}

const post = new Post('Hello world');
JSON.stringify(post);          // '{"title":"Hello world"}'
PostSerializer.serialize(post); // { title: 'Hello world', excerpt: 'Hel' }
```

The explicit list is also a safety feature: fields you don't name (password hashes, internal flags) never reach the API response.

