Inside a static method, `new this(...)` constructs whichever subclass the method was called on. So one pair of static helpers on the base class works for every serializer.

```js
class Serializer {
  static serialize(subject, options) {
    return new this(subject, options).serialize();
  }

  static serializeArray(subjects, options) {
    return subjects.map(subject => this.serialize(subject, options));
  }
}

PostSerializer.serialize(post);        // one object
PostSerializer.serializeArray(posts);  // array of objects
```

Pitfall: `subjects.map(this.serialize)` passes the method without its class, so `this` is lost and `new this()` throws "this is not a constructor". The arrow function keeps `this`.

