`Object.freeze` only locks an object's own properties, and a `Map`'s entries are internal data, not properties, so `set`, `delete` and `clear` keep working. A common workaround is to shadow those methods with `undefined` on the instance and then freeze it, so the properties can't be restored.

```js
const frozenMap = iterable => {
  const m = new Map(iterable);
  m.set = undefined;
  m.delete = undefined;
  m.clear = undefined;
  return Object.freeze(m);
};

const roles = frozenMap([['admin', 1], ['user', 2]]);
roles.get('admin');  // 1
roles.set('x', 3);   // TypeError: roles.set is not a function
```

This is a guard against accidents, not real immutability: `Map.prototype.set.call(roles, 'x', 3)` still adds an entry. If you need a truly read-only lookup, expose only a `get` function, or use a frozen plain object.

