Spread the defaults first and the object second: `{ ...defaults, ...options }`. Later spreads win, so any key the caller supplied is kept and missing keys come from the defaults.

```js
const DEFAULTS = { page: 1, pageSize: 20, sort: 'createdAt' };

const query = { ...DEFAULTS, ...{ pageSize: 50 } };
// { page: 1, pageSize: 50, sort: 'createdAt' }

// Several default objects, where the FIRST one wins:
const defaults = (obj, ...defs) =>
  Object.assign({}, obj, ...defs.reverse(), obj);
defaults({ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }, { b: 6 }, { a: 3 }); // { a: 1, b: 2 }
```

Pitfall: a key that is present but `undefined` still overrides the default: `{ ...DEFAULTS, pageSize: undefined }` gives `pageSize: undefined`. If `undefined` should also fall back, use `options.pageSize ?? DEFAULTS.pageSize`. The merge is shallow, so nested objects are not combined.

