Store the derived result in an internal `Signal` and keep it up to date with an internal `Effect` that recomputes when any dependency changes. Delegate `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener` to the internal signal, so an effect can subscribe to the computed value like any other signal.

```js
class ComputedValue extends EventTarget {
  #signal;
  #effect;

  constructor(compute, dependencies = []) {
    super();
    this.#signal = new Signal(compute());
    this.#effect = new Effect(() => {
      this.#signal.value = compute();
    }, dependencies);
  }

  get value() { return this.#signal.value; }
  addEventListener(type, fn) { this.#signal.addEventListener(type, fn); }
  removeEventListener(type, fn) { this.#signal.removeEventListener(type, fn); }
  dispose() { this.#effect.dispose(); }
}

const price = new Signal(10);
const qty = new Signal(2);
const total = new ComputedValue(() => price.value * qty.value, [price, qty]);
new Effect(() => console.log(`Total: ${total.value}`), [total]); // Total: 20
qty.value = 3;                                                    // Total: 30
```

Because the `Signal` only notifies on real changes, effects on `total` don't rerun when the result is unchanged. Real libraries also batch updates to avoid running effects on half-updated values.

