A `Map` is directly iterable: `for (const [k, v] of map)` works, and `map.keys()`, `map.values()` and `map.entries()` return iterators, always in insertion order. A plain object isn't iterable, so you first build an array with `Object.keys()`, `Object.values()` or `Object.entries()`.

```js
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(obj)) console.log(key, value);

const map = new Map([['a', 1], ['b', 2]]);
for (const [key, value] of map) console.log(key, value);

[...map.entries()]; // [['a', 1], ['b', 2]]  (same as [...map])
```

Map iterators are lazy (no array is built until you spread them). Object key order has one quirk that `Map` doesn't: integer-like keys are listed first, in ascending order.

