A query string is, in essence, a **list of key-value pairs**. They are separated from the URL with the `?` character and from each other with the `&` character. Given a `URL` object, we can use `URL.prototype.search` or `URL.prototype.searchParams`. However, we might not always be able to use the `URL` object, so it's a safe bet to create a function that can work with **plain strings**.

Knowing the delimiter, we can use `String.prototype.split()` to get the appropriate part of the string. Then, using the `URLSearchParams()` constructor, we can create an object that we can convert to an array of key-value pairs using the spread operator (`...`). Finally, we can use `Object.fromEntries()` to convert the array of key-value pairs into an object.

```js
const queryStringToObject = url =>
  Object.fromEntries([...new URLSearchParams(url.split('?')[1])]);

queryStringToObject('https://google.com?page=1&count=10');
// {page: '1', count: '10'}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/query-string-to-object.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Parse it with `URLSearchParams` and convert the resulting pairs with `Object.fromEntries`.

```js
const queryStringToObject = url =>
  Object.fromEntries(new URLSearchParams(url.split('?')[1]));

queryStringToObject('https://shop.test/products?page=1&count=10');
// { page: '1', count: '10' }

// With a full URL (or location.href) you can let URL do the splitting:
Object.fromEntries(new URL('https://shop.test/p?page=1').searchParams); // { page: '1' }
```

`URLSearchParams` also decodes `%20` and `+` for you, which a hand-written `split('&')` would miss.

Two pitfalls: every value is a **string** (`'1'`, not `1`), so convert numbers before doing maths with them. And repeated keys (`?tag=a&tag=b`) collapse to the last one; use `params.getAll('tag')` when a key can repeat.

