Drop the empty entries, then hand the rest to `URLSearchParams`, which encodes every key and value for you.

```js
const objectToQueryString = params => {
  const entries = Object.entries(params).filter(([, v]) => v != null);
  const qs = new URLSearchParams(entries).toString();
  return qs ? `?${qs}` : '';
};

objectToQueryString({ page: 1, q: 'red shoes & socks', sort: undefined });
// '?page=1&q=red+shoes+%26+socks'
```

Gluing strings by hand (`` `${key}=${val}` ``) breaks as soon as a value contains `&`, `=`, `#` or a space. An `&` in a search box would split the value into a bogus extra parameter. `URLSearchParams` encodes spaces as `+`, which servers decode as a space.

Pitfall: `new URLSearchParams({ sort: undefined })` gives `'sort=undefined'`, so filter out empty values first.

