It copies the object's own enumerable top-level properties into a new object. Primitive values are copied, but nested objects and arrays are **shared**: the clone holds the same references.

```js
const settings = { theme: 'dark', tags: ['a'] };
const copy = { ...settings };    // same as Object.assign({}, settings)

copy.theme = 'light';
settings.theme;                  // 'dark' (top level is independent)

copy.tags.push('b');
settings.tags;                   // ['a', 'b'] (nested array is shared!)
```

This is why a React state update like `{ ...state, user: { ...state.user, name } }` spreads each level it changes. For a full independent copy, use `structuredClone(obj)`.

