A static method belongs to the class itself and is called on it (`Array.isArray(x)`). An instance method lives on the class's prototype and is called on an instance (`arr.push(4)`), where it can use that instance's data through `this`.

```js
class User {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  greet() { return `Hi, ${this.name}`; }              // instance
  static fromJSON(json) { return new User(json.name); } // static
}

const u = User.fromJSON({ name: 'Ada' });
u.greet();        // 'Hi, Ada'
// u.fromJSON     -> undefined (static methods are not on instances)
// User.greet     -> undefined
```

Use `static` for helpers that do not need one particular instance: factories (`User.fromJSON`, `Date.now()`), validators (`Number.isInteger`), or utilities (`Object.keys`).

