With a regular `function`, `this` is the element the listener is attached to. With an arrow function, `this` is whatever it was in the surrounding code (often `undefined` or the component), not the element. The reliable option that works in both is `event.currentTarget`.

```js
button.addEventListener('click', function () {
  this === button;                  // true
});

button.addEventListener('click', event => {
  event.currentTarget === button;   // true (element the listener is on)
  event.target;                     // element actually clicked, e.g. an <svg> icon inside the button
});
```

Prefer `event.currentTarget`: it does not depend on the function style, and it reads clearly. Use `event.target` when you delegate one listener on a parent (such as a `<ul>`) to handle clicks on many children.

