Given an **object with an array-valued property**, you might want to **unwind** it into an array of objects. This is a common operation when working with data that has been aggregated or grouped.

For starters, you have to exclude the key-value pair for the specified `key` from the object. You can use **object destructuring** for this purpose. Then, you can use `Array.prototype.map()` for the values of the given `key` to create an **array of objects**. Each object contains the original object's values, except for `key` which is mapped to its individual values.

```js
const unwind = (key, obj) => {
  const { [key]: _, ...rest } = obj;
  return obj[key].map(val => ({ ...rest, [key]: val }));
};

unwind('b', { a: true, b: [1, 2] });
// [{ a: true, b: 1 }, { a: true, b: 2 }]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/unwind-object.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Pull the array field out with destructuring, then `map` over the array, spreading the remaining fields into each new object together with a single element.

```js
const unwind = (key, obj) => {
  const { [key]: _, ...rest } = obj;
  return obj[key].map(val => ({ ...rest, [key]: val }));
};

unwind('items', { orderId: 1, items: ['pen', 'ink'] });
// [{ orderId: 1, items: 'pen' }, { orderId: 1, items: 'ink' }]
```

`{ [key]: _, ...rest }` uses a computed key to remove whichever field name is passed in. This flattening turns grouped data into rows, for example to export an order with its line items to CSV or a table. It matches MongoDB's `$unwind` stage and is roughly the reverse of grouping. Pitfall: if the field is missing or not an array, `obj[key].map` throws, so guard it with `?? []` when the input is untrusted.

