Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/use-strict.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
In strict code, `eval` gets its own variable scope. `var` and function declarations inside the evaluated string stay inside that `eval` and never leak into or overwrite the surrounding scope.

```js
// sloppy script
var x = 1;
eval('var x = 3');
x; // 3 (eval leaked into the outer scope)

// strict ('use strict' only works at the top of a script or function)
function strict() {
  'use strict';
  var y = 1;
  eval('var y = 3');
  return y;
}
strict(); // 1 (eval's var stayed inside eval)
```

`let`/`const` inside `eval` are block-scoped to the eval in both modes; the difference is `var`. In practice: avoid `eval` altogether. It is slow, blocked by most Content Security Policies, and a code-injection risk when it touches user input. `JSON.parse` covers parsing data safely.

