back:
Write the group as `(?<name>...)`; the captured text appears on `match.groups.name`. Names make code readable and don't break when you add another group.

```js
const m = '2024-03-15'.match(/(?<year>\d{4})-(?<month>\d{2})-(?<day>\d{2})/);
m.groups; // { year: '2024', month: '03', day: '15' }

const { year, month } = m.groups; // destructuring works

// In replace(), refer to it as $<name>
'2024-03-15'.replace(/(?<y>\d{4})-(?<m>\d{2})-(?<d>\d{2})/, '$<d>/$<m>/$<y>');
// '15/03/2024'
```

Named groups are still numbered too (`m[1]` is `'2024'`). If there is no match, `match()` returns `null`, so check before reading `.groups`.

