Wrap each part you want in parentheses (capturing groups). `exec()`/`match()` returns an array where index 0 is the whole match and 1, 2, … are the groups in order of their opening parenthesis.

```js
const [, key, value] = /(\w+)=(\w+)/.exec('color=red');
key;   // 'color'
value; // 'red'

// With the g flag, match() drops the groups; use matchAll() instead
'a=1&b=2'.match(/(\w+)=(\w+)/g); // ['a=1', 'b=2']
[...'a=1&b=2'.matchAll(/(\w+)=(\w+)/g)].map(m => [m[1], m[2]]);
// [['a', '1'], ['b', '2']]
```

`exec()`/`match()` return `null` when nothing matches, so guard before destructuring real user input.

