It writes ANSI escape codes: `\x1b[` + a number + `m`. `30`–`37` set the text color (31 is red), `40`–`47` the background, and `0` resets everything. The terminal keeps a color on until something resets it, so without `\x1b[0m` the color leaks into everything printed afterwards.

```js
import { styleText } from 'node:util';

const red = text => `\x1b[31m${text}\x1b[0m`;
console.log(red('Error:'), 'file not found'); // only "Error:" is red

// Node 22: built-in helper that adds the reset for you
console.log(styleText('red', 'Error:'));
```

Prefer `util.styleText` (or a library like `chalk`) in real code: it also skips the codes when output is not a terminal (e.g. piped into a log file), where raw codes show up as garbage.

