Parse it with `new URL()` and edit `url.searchParams` (`set`, `append`, `delete`) and `url.hash`, then turn it back into a string. The URL object handles `?`/`&` placement and percent-encoding for you, which hand-built string concatenation often gets wrong.

```js
const url = new URL('https://shop.example.com/products?page=2&from=home#reviews');

url.searchParams.delete('from');
url.searchParams.set('page', 3);                  // replaces the existing value
url.searchParams.set('q', 'red shoes & socks');   // encoded for you
url.hash = '';

url.href;
// 'https://shop.example.com/products?page=3&q=red+shoes+%26+socks'
```

`set()` replaces all values for a key; `append()` adds another one (for `?tag=a&tag=b`). `new URL()` throws on a relative string like `'/products'`, so pass a base: `new URL('/products', location.origin)`.

