Split on `;`, then split each pair at its first `=` using `indexOf`, trim, and `decodeURIComponent` both sides. Values can legally contain `=` (Base64 tokens end in `==`), so `pair.split('=')` would cut them off.

```js
const parseCookie = str =>
  Object.fromEntries(
    str.split(';').filter(Boolean).map(pair => {
      const i = pair.indexOf('=');
      return [
        decodeURIComponent(pair.slice(0, i).trim()),
        decodeURIComponent(pair.slice(i + 1).trim()),
      ];
    })
  );

parseCookie('theme=dark; token=abc%3D%3D; eq=E=mc2');
// { theme: 'dark', token: 'abc==', eq: 'E=mc2' }
parseCookie(''); // {} (document.cookie is '' when there are no cookies)
```

The common `v.split('=')` + `v[1]` version returns `'abc'` for `'token=abc=='` and throws on an empty string. On a Node server, frameworks parse the `Cookie` header for you (e.g. `req.cookies` with cookie-parser).

