Pick `length` random characters from an alphabet. Use `crypto.getRandomValues()` (available in browsers and Node) for the randomness whenever the string guards anything: `Math.random()` is predictable and must not be used for tokens, reset codes or session ids.

```js
const randomAlphaNumeric = length => {
  const chars = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789';
  const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(length));
  return Array.from(bytes, b => chars[b % chars.length]).join('');
};

randomAlphaNumeric(8); // e.g. 'k3x9q0za'

// Quick, NOT secure: fine for a temporary DOM id
Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 10);
```

`b % 36` is very slightly biased (256 is not a multiple of 36); acceptable for ids. For unique ids, `crypto.randomUUID()` is simpler still.

