No, strings are immutable primitives: no operation changes a string in place; methods like `replace`, `slice` and `toUpperCase` return a *new* string. Assigning to an index does nothing in sloppy mode and throws a `TypeError` in strict mode (which ES modules and classes always use).

```js
'use strict';
