x[1] = 'a'; // TypeError: Cannot assign to read only property '1'

// Build a new string instead
const y = x.slice(0, 1) + 'a' + x.slice(2); // 'tape'

let s = 'gem';
const t = s;
s = s.replace('e', 'y'); // s is 'gym'; t is still 'gem'
```

Java, C# and Python strings are immutable too. The practical consequence: always use the return value (`name = name.trim()`); calling `name.trim()` on its own line does nothing.

