Try the token's suffixes from longest to shortest: `word.slice(-i)` for `i` from `word.length` down to 1, and return the first one the text `startsWith`. Longest-first means you get the biggest overlap.

```js
const startsWithSubstring = (text, word) => {
  for (let i = word.length; i > 0; i--) {
    const suffix = word.slice(-i);
    if (text.startsWith(suffix)) return suffix;
  }
  return undefined;
};

startsWithSubstring('/>next chunk', '<br />'); // '/>'
startsWithSubstring('>x', '<br />');           // '>'
startsWithSubstring('x', '<br />');            // undefined
```

`slice(-i)` returns the last `i` characters. As with any loop, declare `i` with `let`; the widely copied `for (i = ...)` version leaks a global and fails in strict mode.

