back:
Create an `AbortController`, pass its `signal` in the `fetch()` options, and call `controller.abort()`. The fetch promise then rejects with a `DOMException` whose `name` is `'AbortError'`.

```js
const controller = new AbortController();

fetch('/api/search?q=rea', { signal: controller.signal })
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(showResults)
  .catch(err => {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') return; // we cancelled it on purpose
    showError(err);
  });

controller.abort(); // e.g. the user typed another letter
```

Pitfall: an abort is a rejection, so treat `AbortError` as "ignore", not as a real failure. For a time limit, pass `AbortSignal.timeout(5000)` as the signal; it rejects with a `'TimeoutError'` instead. A controller can only be aborted once, so create a new one per request.

