Yes. The one difference is `this`. A regular `function` listener gets `this` set to the element the listener is attached to. An arrow function has no `this` of its own, so it uses the `this` of the surrounding code, which is not the element.

```js
button.addEventListener('click', function () {
  this.disabled = true;          // `this` is the button
});

button.addEventListener('click', event => {
  event.currentTarget.disabled = true; // use the event instead of `this`
});
```

In practice, arrow callbacks are fine and common. Read the element from `event.currentTarget` (the element the listener is on) or `event.target` (the element that was actually clicked) instead of relying on `this`.

