It throws `TypeError: X is not a constructor`. Arrow functions have no `this` of their own and no `prototype` property, so JavaScript cannot build an object with them. Regular functions and classes can be used with `new`.

```js
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
new User('Ada');      // User { name: 'Ada' }

const UserArrow = name => { this.name = name; };
new UserArrow('Ada'); // TypeError: UserArrow is not a constructor
```

In modern code, write constructors as `class User { constructor(name) { this.name = name; } }`. Keep arrow functions for callbacks and small helpers.

