A promise starts **pending**. It then becomes either **fulfilled** with a value or **rejected** with a reason (usually an `Error`). "Settled" means fulfilled or rejected, and a settled promise never changes again: later `resolve`/`reject` calls are ignored.

```js
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  resolve('first');
  reject(new Error('ignored')); // no effect: already settled
});

p.then(value => console.log(value)); // 'first'
```

This is why you can attach `.then()` late, or several times, and still get the same result. The value is remembered once the promise has settled.

