An `async` function always returns a promise. The value you `return` fulfills that promise, and an error you `throw` rejects it. `await` pauses the function until a promise settles. If the promise rejects, `await` throws, so you handle failures with `try...catch`.

```js
async function getUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();   // fulfills getUser's promise
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return null;
  }
}

getUser(1).then(user => console.log(user)); // always a promise
```

`await` works inside `async` functions and at the top level of ES modules. Even `await` on a non-promise value pauses until later in the same turn: the rest of the function runs as a microtask (a queued job that runs right after the current code finishes).

