While `try...catch` blocks are commonly used to handle errors, they're not particularly friendly to the **functional programming** style. Luckily, we can roll up our own utility function to make it easier to handle errors when invoking functions.

All that we need is a [higher-order function](https://www.30secondsofcode.org/js/s/higher-order-functions) that takes a function and its arguments. It then uses a `try...catch` block to return either the **result** of the function or an appropriate **error** object. If the caught object is not an `Error`, it creates a new `Error` object.

```js
const attempt = (fn, ...args) => {
  try {
    return fn(...args);
  } catch (e) {
    return e instanceof Error ? e : new Error(e);
  }
};

let elements = attempt(function(selector) {
  return document.querySelectorAll(selector);
}, '>_>');
if (elements instanceof Error) elements = []; // elements = []
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/attempt-invoking-function.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
It calls `fn` inside `try...catch`. It returns the result on success and an `Error` object on failure, wrapping non-`Error` throws with `new Error(e)` so callers always get an `Error`.

```js
const attempt = (fn, ...args) => {
  try {
    return fn(...args);
  } catch (e) {
    return e instanceof Error ? e : new Error(e);
  }
};

const data = attempt(JSON.parse, userInput);
if (data instanceof Error) showMessage('Invalid JSON');
```

Trade-off: the error no longer stops the code, so every caller must remember to check `instanceof Error`. Forgetting that check silently passes an `Error` object along as if it were data.

