Hidden state is a variable captured in a closure that outside code can't see or reset directly. You can only reach it through the returned functions. Because the result of `counter.increment()` depends on every earlier call, you can't predict it by reading that line alone.

```js
const initCounter = (start = 0) => {
  let value = start; // hidden, mutable
  return {
    get: () => value,
    increment: () => ++value,
    reset: () => (value = start),
  };
};

const counter = initCounter(5);
counter.increment(); // 6
counter.increment(); // 7
```

It becomes a problem when many parts of the app share and mutate the same closure, because bugs then depend on call order. Encapsulation itself is often what you want. When it isn't, pass the state in and return the new state, like a React reducer.

