Return a wrapper that collects all arguments and forwards only `args.slice(0, n)`. It stops extra arguments, such as the index and array that `map` passes, from leaking into functions that accept many arguments.

```js
const nAry = (fn, n) => (...args) => fn(...args.slice(0, n));

const firstTwoMax = nAry(Math.max, 2);
[[2, 6, 'a'], [6, 4, 8], [10]].map(x => firstTwoMax(...x));
// [6, 6, 10]  ('a' and 8 are ignored)
```

Real-world version: `items.map(x => fn(x))` is the everyday way to limit a callback to one argument.

