Many functions accept optional or variable arguments, and callers such as `map`, `forEach`, and event listeners pass more arguments than you might expect. Fixing the arity (the number of arguments a function takes) makes sure only the arguments you intend reach the function. Currying helpers also rely on it, because they read `fn.length`.

```js
['a', 'b'].forEach(console.log);
// a 0 [ 'a', 'b' ]
// b 1 [ 'a', 'b' ]   (index and array leak in)

['a', 'b'].forEach(x => console.log(x));
// a
// b
```

`nullary`, `unary`, `binary`, and `nAry` are small helpers for this. An inline arrow like `x => fn(x)` does the same job.

