`bindKey` looks up `obj[key]` each time it is called, not when it is created. So if the method is later replaced, the bound function uses the new version. `bind` freezes the function that existed at bind time.

```js
const bindKey = (context, key, ...boundArgs) => (...args) =>
  context[key].apply(context, [...boundArgs, ...args]);

const fred = {
  user: 'fred',
  greet(greeting) { return `${greeting} ${this.user}`; },
};
const late = bindKey(fred, 'greet');
const early = fred.greet.bind(fred);

fred.greet = function (g) { return `${g.toUpperCase()} ${this.user}`; };
late('hi');  // 'HI fred'
early('hi'); // 'hi fred'
```

This matters when methods get patched or mocked in tests after callbacks were created.

