back:
`apply` takes exactly two arguments: the `this` value and one array (or array-like) of arguments. `fn.apply(obj, args)` works. `fn.apply(obj, ...args)` passes the first element as that "array", so it throws a `TypeError` when that element isn't array-like.

```js
function printThisAndData(...data) {
  console.log(this.data, ...data);
}
const obj = { data: 0 };
const data = [1, 2, 3];

printThisAndData.apply(obj, data);    // 0 1 2 3
printThisAndData.apply(obj, ...data); // TypeError: CreateListFromArrayLike called on non-object
printThisAndData.call(obj, ...data);  // 0 1 2 3 (modern equivalent)
```

Memory hook: **a**pply takes an **a**rray, **c**all takes **c**omma-separated arguments.

