Replace each method with a bound copy: `obj[name] = obj[name].bind(obj)`. The bound function always runs with `this === obj`, no matter how it is called, and it still forwards its arguments.

```js
const bindAll = (obj, ...names) => {
  for (const name of names) obj[name] = obj[name].bind(obj);
};

const view = {
  label: 'docs',
  click() { console.log('clicked ' + this.label); },
};
bindAll(view, 'click');
document.body.addEventListener('click', view.click); // 'clicked docs'
```

Pitfall: a hand-rolled version that stores the method in a shared variable, such as `f = obj[fn]` in a `forEach`, makes every method call the last one captured and drops arguments. `bind` avoids both problems.

