The logical operations we've seen so far work with **boolean values**, but what if we want to use them with **functions**? For example, what if we want to check if both of two functions return `true` for a given set of arguments? We'll have to alter them in such a way that they work with functions. For example, here's the implementation of the logical and, or, and not operators for functions:

```js
// Logical and for functions
const both = (f, g) => (...args) => f(...args) && g(...args);
// Logical or for functions
const either = (f, g) => (...args) => f(...args) || g(...args);
// Logical not for functions
const complement = fn => (...args) => !fn(...args);
```

In fact, we can generalize this to implement any binary logical operator for functions, using [higher-order functions](https://www.30secondsofcode.org/js/s/higher-order-functions).

```js
const logicalOperator =
  operatorFn =>
  (f, g) =>
  (...args) =>
    operatorFn(f(...args), g(...args));

const and = (a, b) => a && b;
const or = (a, b) => a || b;

const both = logicalOperator(and);
const either = logicalOperator(or);

const isEven = num => num % 2 === 0;
const isPositive = num => num > 0;
const isPositiveAndEven = both(isEven, isPositive);
const isPositiveOrEven = either(isEven, isPositive);

isPositiveAndEven(4); // true
isPositiveAndEven(-2); // false
isPositiveOrEven(4); // true
isPositiveOrEven(-2); // true
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/logical-operations.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Write higher-order helpers that take two predicates and return a new predicate. The new function calls both with the same arguments and joins the results with `&&` or `||`. A predicate is a function that returns true or false.

```js
const both = (f, g) => (...args) => f(...args) && g(...args);
const either = (f, g) => (...args) => f(...args) || g(...args);
const complement = fn => (...args) => !fn(...args);

const isEven = n => n % 2 === 0;
const isPositive = n => n > 0;

both(isEven, isPositive)(4);    // true
both(isEven, isPositive)(-2);   // false
either(isEven, isPositive)(-2); // true

products.filter(both(isInStock, isOnSale));
```

`&&` and `||` short-circuit, so `g` is not called when `f` already decides the answer.

