Resolve the requested path to an absolute path, then reject it unless it still starts with the public folder plus a path separator. For extra safety, only serve an allowlist of file extensions.

```js
import { resolve, sep, extname } from 'node:path';

const root = resolve(import.meta.dirname, 'public');
const allowed = ['html', 'css', 'js', 'json', 'png'];

function safePath(requestUrl) {
  const filePath = resolve(root, '.' + requestUrl);
  if (!filePath.startsWith(root + sep)) return null;   // escaped the folder
  if (!allowed.includes(extname(filePath).slice(1))) return null;
  return filePath;
}

safePath('/css/site.css');  // '.../public/css/site.css'
safePath('/../../.env');    // null
```

Pitfall: `filePath.startsWith(root)` alone is not enough, because `/srv/public-old/secret.txt` also starts with `/srv/public`. Compare against `root + sep`. If you decode the URL (`%2e%2e` is `..`), run the check after decoding.

