In order to partially apply a function by prepending arguments, you can use the spread operator (`...`) to prepend `partials` to the list of arguments of `fn`. Simply pass `partials` **before any other arguments** that are supplied.

```js
const partial = (fn, ...partials) => (...args) => fn(...partials, ...args);

const greet = (greeting, name) => greeting + ' ' + name + '!';
const greetHello = partial(greet, 'Hello');
greetHello('John'); // 'Hello John!'
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/partially-apply-function.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Return a new function that calls `fn` with the preset arguments first, followed by whatever the caller passes. `fn.bind(null, ...presetArgs)` does the same thing natively.

```js
const partial = (fn, ...partials) => (...args) => fn(...partials, ...args);

const greet = (greeting, name) => greeting + ' ' + name + '!';
const greetHello = partial(greet, 'Hello');
greetHello('John'); // 'Hello John!'

const greetHi = greet.bind(null, 'Hi');
greetHi('Ann');     // 'Hi Ann!'
```

This is called partial application: you fix some arguments now and supply the rest later. It is handy for config-like arguments, such as `const logError = partial(log, 'error')`. `bind` also fixes `this` (the first argument), so pass `null` when the function doesn't use `this`.

