JavaScript **promises are asynchronous**, meaning that they are executed in parallel. This is great for performance, but sometimes you need to execute promises one after another (**sequentially**). This can be easily accomplished by **chaining promises** together, using `Array.prototype.reduce()`. Each promise in the chain returns the next promise when resolved, using `Promise.prototype.then()`.

```js
const runPromisesInSeries = ps =>
  ps.reduce((p, next) => p.then(next), Promise.resolve());

const delay = d => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, d));
runPromisesInSeries([() => delay(1000), () => delay(2000)]);
// Executes each promise sequentially, taking a total of 3 seconds to complete
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/run-promises-in-series.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Keep a list of functions that each start a task, and only call the next one after the previous promise resolves: with `for...of` plus `await`, or by chaining them with `reduce`.

```js
const delay = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
const tasks = [() => delay(1000), () => delay(2000)];

// Readable version
for (const task of tasks) await task(); // ~3s total

// reduce version
const runPromisesInSeries = fns =>
  fns.reduce((p, next) => p.then(next), Promise.resolve());
await runPromisesInSeries(tasks);       // ~3s total
```

A promise doesn't "run": the work starts the moment you call the function that creates it. So `[delay(1000), delay(2000)]` has already started both timers, and waiting on them in order would take only ~2s. Wrapping each task in `() => ...` delays the start. Use series when order matters or to avoid hammering an API; use `Promise.all` when tasks are independent.

