Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/modeling-money-currency-exchange-rates.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`Intl.supportedValuesOf('currency')` returns the ISO 4217 codes (`'EUR'`, `'USD'`, ...). For each code, build a function that formats values with `Intl.NumberFormat` and `style: 'currency'`, and store the results in a `Map` keyed by code.

```js
const isoCodes = Intl.supportedValuesOf('currency');

const allCurrencies = new Map(
  isoCodes.map(code => {
    const format = (display = 'symbol') => value =>
      new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
        style: 'currency', currency: code, currencyDisplay: display,
      }).format(value);
    return [code, Object.freeze({ code, format })];
  })
);

allCurrencies.get('USD').format()(1000);       // '$1,000.00'
allCurrencies.get('EUR').format('name')(1000); // '1,000.00 euros'
allCurrencies.get('JPY').format()(1000);       // '¥1,000' (no decimals)
```

`Object.freeze` stops a currency object from being changed by accident. `Intl` knows each currency's decimal places (yen has none), which a hand-rolled `'$' + n.toFixed(2)` gets wrong. The locale decides the symbol position and separators. Pass the user's locale, not `'en-US'`, in a real app.

