back:
A rate belongs to a **pair** of currencies in one direction, not to a single currency, and it changes over time. One object that maps `from→to` keys to rates is easy to update, to replace wholesale (for example one `Bank` per date for historical rates), and to pass around.

```js
// Currency.wrap accepts 'usd' or a currency object and returns the canonical object
const pairKey = (from, to) => `${Currency.wrap(from).code}->${Currency.wrap(to).code}`;

class Bank {
  rates = new Map();
  setRate(from, to, rate) { this.rates.set(pairKey(from, to), rate); }
  getRate(from, to) { return this.rates.get(pairKey(from, to)); }
}

const bank = new Bank();
bank.setRate('usd', 'eur', 0.85);
bank.getRate('USD', 'EUR'); // 0.85
bank.getRate('EUR', 'USD'); // undefined (each direction is stored separately)
```

Rates are directional: real buy and sell rates include a spread, so EUR→USD is not exactly `1 / 0.85`. Store both directions explicitly instead of inverting.

