back:
Look up the rate for the `money.currency → to` pair and return a **new** `Money` whose value is `money.value * rate`. If the currencies already match, return the money unchanged. If no rate is known, throw an error rather than guessing.

```js
exchange(money, to) {
  const toCurrency = Currency.wrap(to);           // 'eur' -> EUR currency object
  if (toCurrency === money.currency) return money;

  const rate = this.getRate(money.currency, toCurrency);
  if (!rate)
    throw new RangeError(`No rate ${money.currency.code} -> ${toCurrency.code}`);
  return new Money(money.value * rate, toCurrency);
}

bank.setRate('usd', 'eur', 0.85);
bank.exchange(new Money(1000, 'usd'), 'eur').format(); // '€850.00'
```

The `===` check works because each currency is a single shared object. Returning a new `Money` (never mutating the original) keeps values safe to share. Real money code should avoid float maths such as `19.99 * 3`: work in integer minor units (cents) or use a decimal library, and round only at defined points.

