Convert `other` into this money's currency first (if the currencies differ), then add the raw values and return a **new** `Money` in this currency. The same "convert, then compare or compute" step applies to `subtract`, `equals`, `greaterThan` and so on.

```js
class Money {
  // ...
  add(other) {
    if (this.currency !== other.currency) other = other.exchangeTo(this.currency);
    return new Money(this.value + other.value, this.currency);
  }
  greaterThan(other) {
    if (this.currency !== other.currency) other = other.exchangeTo(this.currency);
    return this.value > other.value;
  }
}

bank.setRate('eur', 'usd', 1.18);
const usd = new Money(1000, 'usd');
const eur = new Money(500, 'eur');
usd.add(eur).format();    // '$1,590.00'
usd.greaterThan(eur);     // true
```

The result is in the left operand's currency, so `eur.add(usd)` is in euros.

Pitfalls: floats drift (`0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3`), so use integer cents or a decimal library and round deliberately. `equals` on converted values is fragile. And the two directions (0.85 vs 1.18) are not exact inverses.

