back:
`=` stores the right-hand value into the variable or property on the left, and the whole assignment is itself an expression that evaluates to that value. So `if (status = 'done')` overwrites `status` and then tests `'done'`, which is always truthy, instead of comparing.

```js
let x;
console.log(x = 5); // 5 (assignment returns the value)

let status = 'pending';
if (status = 'done') { /* always runs, status is now 'done' */ }
if (status === 'done') { /* correct: compare with === */ }

const limit = 10;
limit = 20; // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.
```

Use `===` to compare. Only `let` variables (and object properties) can be reassigned; a `const` binding cannot.

