back:
Use spread syntax (`...`) to build a new copy: `[...arr, item]` for arrays and `{ ...obj, field: value }` for objects. The original stays unchanged, which is what React needs to notice the update.

```js
const todos = ['a', 'b'];
const next = [...todos, 'c'];          // ['a', 'b', 'c']

const user = { id: 7, role: 'viewer' };
const admin = { ...user, role: 'admin' }; // { id: 7, role: 'admin' }
```

Array spread works on any iterable (arrays, strings, Sets, Maps). Object spread is different: it copies an object's own enumerable properties, and a plain object is not iterable, so `[...{ a: 1 }]` throws a TypeError. Later keys win, so put the override after the spread. Both are shallow copies: nested objects are still shared.

