`i++` (postfix) sets `j` to `0`, the old value; `++i` (prefix) sets `j` to `1`, the new value. Both increment `i` to `1`. The difference only matters when you use the expression's value; as a standalone statement such as `i++;` in a loop, they are identical.

```js
let i = 0;
let j = i++;  // j = 0, i = 1  (postfix: returns old value)

let n = 0;
let m = ++n;  // m = 1, n = 1  (prefix: returns new value)

let k = n--;  // k = 1, n = 0  (-- works the same way)
```

Relying on the returned value, as in `arr[i++] = x`, is easy to misread. Many teams write `i += 1` on its own line instead.

