back:
Ask `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for the date's parts **in the shop's time zone** (`timeZone: 'Europe/Athens'`) with `formatToParts()`, then check the weekday, hour and month-day against your rules. `getHours()`/`getDay()` won't do: they use the runtime's own time zone.

```js
const holidays = new Set(['1-1', '3-25', '12-25']); // month-day in the shop's zone

const partsIn = (date, timeZone) => Object.fromEntries(
  new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
    timeZone, weekday: 'short', month: 'numeric', day: 'numeric',
    hour: 'numeric', hourCycle: 'h23',
  }).formatToParts(date).map(p => [p.type, p.value])
);

const isInsideBusinessHours = (date = new Date(), timeZone = 'Europe/Athens') => {
  const { weekday, month, day, hour } = partsIn(date, timeZone);
  const h = Number(hour);
  return !['Sat', 'Sun'].includes(weekday) &&
    !holidays.has(`${month}-${day}`) && h >= 9 && h < 17;
};

isInsideBusinessHours(new Date('2024-06-03T07:30:00Z')); // true (Mon 10:30 in Athens)
isInsideBusinessHours(new Date('2024-06-01T09:00:00Z')); // false (Saturday)
```

Use `hourCycle: 'h23'` rather than `hour12: false`; some engines format midnight as `24` with the latter. Pin the locale (`'en-US'`) so the weekday names you compare against are predictable.

