```js
const isISOString = val => {
  const d = new Date(val);
  return !Number.isNaN(d.valueOf()) && d.toISOString() === val;
};

isISOString('2020-10-12T10:10:10.000Z');      // true
isISOString('2020-10-12');                    // false
isISOString('2020-10-12T10:10:10Z');          // false (no milliseconds)
isISOString('2020-10-12T12:10:10.000+02:00'); // false (offset, not Z)
```

`toISOString()` always outputs `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss.sssZ`, so this is strict: it validates "exactly this format", not "any valid ISO 8601". For API input, a schema library or a regex is often clearer about what you accept.

