Take the sign off first, then split the absolute value into hours (`/ 3600`), minutes (`/ 60 % 60`) and seconds (`% 60`), floor each part and pad it to two digits.

```js
const formatSeconds = s => {
  const sign = s < 0 ? '-' : '';
  const abs = Math.abs(s);
  return sign + [abs / 3_600, (abs / 60) % 60, abs % 60]
    .map(v => `${Math.floor(v)}`.padStart(2, '0'))
    .join(':');
};

formatSeconds(200);    // '00:03:20'
formatSeconds(-200);   // '-00:03:20'
formatSeconds(99_999); // '27:46:39' (hours can exceed 24)
formatSeconds(0);      // '00:00:00'
```

Handling the sign up front avoids a classic bug: a version that branches on `s > 0` prints `'-00:00:00'` for zero. This is a clock-style duration, not an ISO 8601 duration (which looks like `PT3M20S`).

