Wrap `setTimeout` in a Promise: `const sleep = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms))`. `await sleep(300)` pauses **only the current async function**; the event loop keeps running, so the page stays responsive and other code runs meanwhile.

```js
const sleep = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

const greet = async () => {
  console.log('I will be with you in just a moment.');
  await sleep(300);
  console.log('Hello there!'); // ~300 ms later
};
greet();
```

Typical use: a delay between retries of a failed request. In Node you can also `import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises'`. Never "sleep" with a busy `while (Date.now() < end)` loop; that really does freeze the thread.

