`off()` needs the **wrapper** that was actually registered (not your original handler) and the same capture flag, so `on()` must return that wrapper. The modern alternative is `AbortController`: pass its `signal` when adding listeners and call `abort()` to remove them all.

```js
const on = (el, type, selector, fn) => {
  const wrapper = e => {
    const match = e.target.closest(selector);
    if (match) fn(e, match);
  };
  el.addEventListener(type, wrapper);
  return wrapper; // off() needs this, not fn
};
const off = (el, type, wrapper) => el.removeEventListener(type, wrapper);

// Or: no function bookkeeping
const controller = new AbortController();
list.addEventListener('click', onClick, { signal: controller.signal });
list.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, { signal: controller.signal });
controller.abort(); // removes both
```

You don't need jQuery for events today: `addEventListener` options (`once`, `capture`, `passive`, `signal`) cover what `.on()`, `.one()` and `.off()` did.

