Loop over the event type names and call `addEventListener()` once per type with the same handler. Inside the handler, `e.type` tells you which event fired.

```js
const addListeners = (el, types, listener, options) =>
  types.forEach(type => el.addEventListener(type, listener, options));

const btn = document.querySelector('#help-btn');
addListeners(btn, ['mouseenter', 'focus'], showTooltip);
addListeners(btn, ['mouseleave', 'blur'], hideTooltip);
```

Pairing mouse events with `focus`/`blur` is an accessibility win: keyboard users get the tooltip too.

Pitfall: some pairs fire together for one user action (`mousedown` and `click` both fire on a click; `input` and `change` both fire when editing a field), so the handler can run twice. Only combine events that represent different ways to trigger the same thing.

