Call `console.time(label)` before the code and `console.timeEnd(label)` after it; it logs the elapsed milliseconds. `console.timeLog(label)` logs an intermediate time without stopping the timer. For precise measurements use `performance.now()` (or `performance.mark()`/`measure()`).

```js
console.time('load users');
const res = await fetch('/api/users');
console.timeLog('load users'); // e.g. load users: 120.4ms
const users = await res.json();
console.timeEnd('load users'); // e.g. load users: 131.9ms

// Manual, more flexible timing
const start = performance.now();
render(users);
console.log(`render took ${performance.now() - start} ms`);
```

The labels must match exactly. A single timing is noisy: for real performance work, measure many runs or use the DevTools Performance panel.

