  const template = document.createElement('template');
  template.innerHTML = html.trim();
  return template.content.firstElementChild;
};

createElement('<div class="card"><p>Hi</p></div>').className; // 'card'
createElement('<tr><td>Ada</td></tr>').tagName;               // 'TR'
```

Why `<template>` rather than a `<div>`: a div drops elements that can't live inside it (like `<tr>`), and its parsed content is live, so images start loading and `onerror` handlers can fire even before you insert it. Template content is inert. Only the first element is returned.

