Create it, set properties (`className`, `textContent`, `dataset`), then insert it with `append()` (end) or `prepend()` (start). `textContent` treats the value as plain text, so user data can't inject HTML.

```js
const list = document.querySelector('#todo-list');

const li = document.createElement('li');
li.className = 'todo';
li.dataset.id = todo.id;          // data-id="…"
li.textContent = todo.title;      // safe: never parsed as HTML
list.append(li);

// Same thing, shorter
list.append(Object.assign(document.createElement('li'), {
  className: 'todo',
  textContent: todo.title
}));
```

Pitfall: element properties and HTML attributes don't always share names (`class` → `className`, `for` → `htmlFor`). For `aria-*` or custom attributes use `setAttribute()`. For many rows, build them first and add them in one `append(...rows)` call.

