User-agent strings are unreliable: they can be spoofed, browsers are reducing the detail in them, and iPadOS Safari reports a **desktop Mac** user agent by default, so an `/iPad/` test misses it. Instead, detect the **capability** you actually care about with media queries.

```js
// Rough heuristic only
const looksMobile = /Mobi|Android|iPhone|iPad/i.test(navigator.userAgent);

// Better: ask about the capability
const touchFirst = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches; // finger, not mouse
const narrow = matchMedia('(max-width: 640px)').matches;
const noHover = matchMedia('(hover: none)').matches;
```

Layout differences belong in CSS media queries, not JavaScript. Use JS checks only for behaviour (e.g. show "tap" vs "click" hints), and remember a device can have both touch and a mouse.

