back:
Pass `{ capture: true }` (or `true`) as the third argument to `addEventListener()`. Capture listeners on ancestors fire first, outermost to innermost, then the button's own listeners, then bubble listeners, innermost to outermost.

```js
form.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('form capture'), { capture: true });
form.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('form bubble'));
button.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('button'));

// Clicking the button logs:
// form capture
// button
// form bubble
```

Capture is useful when an ancestor must see an event before children handle it (e.g. closing an open menu, or logging). To remove a capture listener, pass `{ capture: true }` to `removeEventListener()` as well.

