A task is one unit of work the event loop takes from a task queue and runs **to completion**: running a `<script>`, an event listener callback for a click or keypress, a `setTimeout`/`setInterval` callback. The browser can only render and handle new input **between** tasks, so a task that runs for 2 seconds freezes the page for 2 seconds.

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout (a new task)'), 0);
console.log('sync (current task)');
// sync (current task)
// timeout (a new task)
```

Browsers keep several task queues (e.g. user input can be prioritised); each is FIFO (first in, first out). To keep the UI responsive, split long work into chunks, move it to a Web Worker, or do it on the server.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/event-loop-explained.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Microtasks are small callbacks that run **as soon as the current task (or script) finishes, before the next task and before rendering**. Promise reactions (`.then`/`.catch`, code after `await`), `queueMicrotask()` and `MutationObserver` callbacks are microtasks.

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('task'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('microtask'));
console.log('sync');
// sync
// microtask
// task
```

The microtask queue is drained **completely**, including microtasks added while draining. So a promise chain that keeps scheduling more work can starve rendering and input just like a long synchronous loop. (Microtasks are also called "jobs" in the ECMAScript spec.)

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/event-loop-explained.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

The delay is only a **minimum**. When the timer expires, `fn` is queued as a new task; it runs only after the current task finishes, all microtasks have run, and any earlier tasks have gone.

```js
const start = performance.now();
setTimeout(() => console.log('timer after', performance.now() - start, 'ms'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('promise first'));
for (let i = 0; i < 1e8; i++); // busy loop blocks the task

// promise first
// timer after ~100 ms (depends on the machine), not 0
```

Browsers also clamp deeply nested timers to at least 4 ms and throttle timers in background tabs (often to once per second or less). Node's event loop orders timers and promises the same way but has no rendering step. Never rely on timers for exact timing.

