Oddly enough, there are two ways to get an element's children: `Node.childNodes` and `Node.children`. The difference between the two is that `Node.childNodes` returns **all child nodes**, including text nodes, while `Node.children` returns **only element nodes**.

Depending on our needs, we can use either of these properties to get an element's children, so let's use an argument to decide which one to return.

```js
const getChildren = (el, includeTextNodes = false) =>
  includeTextNodes ? [...el.childNodes] : [...el.children];

getChildren(document.querySelector('ul'));
// [li, li, li]

getChildren(document.querySelector('ul'), true);
// [li, #text, li, #text, li, #text]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/get-ancestors-parents-siblings-children.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`children` contains only **element** nodes; `childNodes` contains **all** child nodes, including text nodes (even whitespace between tags) and comments.

```js
// <ul>
//   <li>a</li>
//   <li>b</li>
//   <li>c</li>
// </ul>
const ul = document.querySelector('ul');

[...ul.children];   // [li, li, li]
[...ul.childNodes]; // [#text, li, #text, li, #text, li, #text]
```

Both are **live**: they update as the DOM changes. Removing items while looping over `ul.children` skips elements, so copy first (`[...ul.children]`) or use `ul.replaceChildren()` to clear a list. In everyday code you almost always want `children`.

