JavaScript's `Window` and `Element` objects provide a ton of useful properties, which can be leveraged for various different things. One such case is calculating the **scroll position** of the current page or a specific element.

Starting with the global `Window` object, we can use the `Window.scrollX` and `Window.scrollY` properties to get the scroll position of the **current page**.

```js
const getWindowScrollPosition = () => ({
  x: window.scrollX,
  y: window.scrollY
});

getWindowScrollPosition(); // {x: 0, y: 200}
```

Subsequently, all **other elements** define the `Element.scrollLeft` and `Element.scrollTop` properties, which can be used to calculate the scroll position. This, for example, can be used to get the scroll position of the `Document` element, or scrollable elements like a `div` or `aside`.

```js
const getScrollPosition = (el = document.documentElement) => ({
  x: el.scrollLeft,
  y: el.scrollTop
});

getScrollPosition(); // {x: 0, y: 200}
getScrollPosition(document.querySelector('aside')); // {x: 0, y: 120}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/get-scroll-position.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
For the page, read `window.scrollX` and `window.scrollY` (pixels scrolled from the left/top). For any scrollable element, read `el.scrollLeft` and `el.scrollTop`.

```js
const getScrollPosition = (el = document.documentElement) => ({
  x: el.scrollLeft,
  y: el.scrollTop
});

window.scrollY;                                   // 200
getScrollPosition();                              // { x: 0, y: 200 }
getScrollPosition(document.querySelector('.chat')); // { x: 0, y: 120 }
```

`document.documentElement` (the `<html>` element) is the page's scrolling element, so it gives the same numbers as `window.scrollX/Y`. Both `scrollTop`/`scrollLeft` are writable, or use `el.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })`.

Pitfall: reading these inside a `scroll` listener runs very often. For "is this element visible yet?" (lazy loading, infinite lists) use `IntersectionObserver` instead of polling scroll positions.

