I've often found myself in need of dynamically modifying CSS on a web page. While it's very easy to alter the styles of individual elements, using `Element.style`, it's not as easy to inject a whole new set of styles into the page. Being able to do so comes particularly handy when working with CSS variables.

Thinking a little outside the box, we might consider **how we usually write CSS** - in a `<style>` tag. This is exactly what we'll be doing in order to inject some new CSS into the page.

Using `Document.createElement()`, we can create a new `style` element and set its type to `text/css`. We can then set its `innerText` to the CSS string we want to inject. Finally, we can use `Document.head` and `Node.appendChild()` to **append the new element** to the document head.

Doing so will make the browser parse the CSS and apply it to the page. The newly created `style` element will be returned, in case you need to keep a **reference** to it.

```js
const injectCSS = css => {
  let el = document.createElement('style');
  el.type = 'text/css';
  el.innerText = css;
  document.head.appendChild(el);
  return el;
};

injectCSS('body { background-color: #000 }');
// '<style type="text/css">body { background-color: #000 }</style>'
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/inject-css.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Create a `<style>` element, put the CSS text in its `textContent`, and append it to `document.head`. The browser parses it immediately; keep the returned element so you can remove it later.

```js
const injectCSS = css => {
  const el = document.createElement('style');
  el.textContent = css;
  document.head.append(el);
  return el;
};

const style = injectCSS('body { background-color: #000 }');
style.remove(); // undo
```

Use `textContent`, not `innerText`: the `innerText` setter turns newlines into `<br>` elements. `type="text/css"` is the default and can be omitted.

Modern alternative: constructable stylesheets, `const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet(); sheet.replaceSync(css); document.adoptedStyleSheets.push(sheet);`.

Pitfalls: never inject CSS built from user input (CSS can leak data via `url()` requests), and a strict Content-Security-Policy may block inline `<style>`. Often toggling a class or setting a CSS variable is all you need.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/inject-css.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

