Checking if `localStorage` is enabled requires some trial and error, quite literally, as we will use a `try...catch` block to test if operations in `localStorage` are supported.

Using `Storage.setItem()` and `Storage.removeItem()` we will **try to store and delete a value** in `localStorage`. If both operations complete successfully, we will return `true`, otherwise we will return `false`.

```js
const isLocalStorageEnabled = () => {
  try {
    const key = `__storage__test`;
    window.localStorage.setItem(key, null);
    window.localStorage.removeItem(key);
    return true;
  } catch (e) {
    return false;
  }
};

isLocalStorageEnabled(); // true, if localStorage is accessible
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/is-local-storage-session-storage-enabled.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Try a real write and delete inside `try...catch`: if both succeed, storage is usable; if anything throws, it isn't. A simple existence check isn't enough because merely *accessing* `localStorage` can throw (`SecurityError` when site data/cookies are blocked), and `setItem` can throw `QuotaExceededError` when storage is full.

```js
const isLocalStorageEnabled = () => {
  try {
    const key = '__storage_test__';
    localStorage.setItem(key, '1');
    localStorage.removeItem(key);
    return true;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
};

isLocalStorageEnabled(); // true when localStorage works
```

The same pattern works for `sessionStorage`. Even when this returns `true`, keep wrapping later `setItem` calls in `try...catch`, since the quota can still fill up.

---
