When working with scroll listeners in JavaScript, one can often run into performance issues. This is because scroll listeners are triggered on **every single scroll event**, which can be quite frequent. Most of the time, such listeners are used for infinite scrolling and lazy loading, meaning that the scroll event won't be intercepted. As such, `Event.preventDefault()` will not be called, giving us an optimization opportunity.

```js
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  // Do something
  // Can't use `preventDefault` here
}, { passive: true });
```

As demonstrated in this code snippet, setting the `passive` option to `true` will enable certain **performance optimizations** in the browser. This way, the browser will know that it can safely skip the event queue and execute the scroll listener immediately. The result is a much smoother experience for the user, as the scroll event will be handled immediately, instead of being queued and handled later.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/passive-scroll-listener-performance.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
No: `scroll` events can't be cancelled, so `passive` changes nothing for them. `passive: true` matters for `touchstart`, `touchmove` and `wheel`. It promises you won't call `preventDefault()`, so the browser can scroll right away instead of waiting for your handler. For `scroll` itself, keep the handler cheap: batch work into one `requestAnimationFrame` per frame, or avoid the listener altogether.

```js
// Worth passive: wheel/touch events could otherwise block scrolling
window.addEventListener('touchmove', onTouch, { passive: true });

// scroll: do at most one update per frame
let ticking = false;
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (ticking) return;
  ticking = true;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => { updateHeader(scrollY); ticking = false; });
});
```

For lazy loading or infinite scroll, `IntersectionObserver` is better than any scroll listener. Chrome and Firefox already treat touch/wheel listeners on `window`/`document`/`body` as passive by default.

