Most of us are familiar with jquery and probably quite a few of us are familiar with the Chrome console's `$` and `$$` shorthands for query selectors. I recently figured out a way to replicate these shorthands in my code, using `Document.querySelector()`, `Document.querySelectorAll()` and `Function.prototype.bind()`. Here's how to do it, just make sure you don't mix them up with jquery if you are still using it:

```js
const $ = document.querySelector.bind(document);
const $$ = document.querySelectorAll.bind(document);

const mainContent = $('.main-content');
const externalLinks = $$('a[target="_blank"]');
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/query-selector-shorthand.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Bind the methods to `document`: `const $ = document.querySelector.bind(document)`. Without `bind`, calling `$('.x')` runs `querySelector` without `document` as its `this`, which throws a `TypeError` ("Illegal invocation" in Chrome).

```js
const $ = document.querySelector.bind(document);
const $$ = (selector, root = document) => [...root.querySelectorAll(selector)];

const form = $('#signup');
const inputs = $$('input', form); // real array: .map/.filter work
```

`querySelectorAll` returns a static `NodeList` (it has `forEach` but not `map`/`filter`), so spreading it into an array is often handy. Don't name these `$` in a codebase that still loads jQuery.

