`innerText` returns the text *as rendered*, so the browser must apply CSS and compute layout (a reflow) before answering. `textContent` just concatenates the text nodes in the DOM with no layout work.

```js
// In a loop over 1,000 rows this forces layout every time
rows.forEach(row => console.log(row.innerText));
// Cheap: no layout needed
rows.forEach(row => console.log(row.textContent));
```

Rule of thumb: use `textContent` by default, for both reading and setting text. Use `innerText` only when you really need the visible text (hidden elements skipped, CSS line breaks applied), and cache the result instead of reading it repeatedly.

