No. The delay is a **minimum**: after it elapses, `fn` is queued as a task and runs only when the call stack is empty and all pending microtasks (e.g. resolved promise callbacks) have run. A long-running script delays it further.

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('microtask'));
console.log('sync');
// sync
// microtask
// timeout
```

JavaScript runs on one main thread, and the event loop only picks the next task when the current code finishes. That's why a heavy loop makes timers (and clicks) feel late. For "run after the current work", `queueMicrotask` is sooner; for UI updates, `requestAnimationFrame` is better timed.

