The **ESLint extension** (`dbaeumer.vscode-eslint`) runs your project's ESLint rules as you type, underlining problems in the editor and listing them in the Problems panel. It uses the project's own config (`eslint.config.js`, the "flat config" that is the default since ESLint 9) and its locally installed `eslint` package.

To auto-fix on save, add this to your VS Code settings:

```json
{
  "editor.codeActionsOnSave": {
    "source.fixAll.eslint": "explicit"
  }
}
```

Only fixable rules are fixed (e.g. `prefer-const`, unused `eslint-disable` comments). Logic problems such as `no-undef` still need you. If nothing shows up, check that `eslint` is installed in the project (`npm i -D eslint`) and look at the ESLint output channel for config errors.

