Wrap `setTimeout` in a `new Promise` and pass the promise's `resolve` function as the timer callback. The promise resolves when the timer fires, so `await` pauses the async function for that long.

```js
const sleep = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

const run = async () => {
  console.log('Saving...');
  await sleep(300); // pauses this function only, not the whole page
  console.log('Done');
};
run();
```

Pitfall: write `setTimeout(resolve, ms)`, not `setTimeout(resolve(), ms)`. The second calls `resolve` immediately, so there is no delay.

In Node you can import a ready-made version: `import { setTimeout as sleep } from 'node:timers/promises';`. Useful for retry back-off, or fake latency in a demo.

