Race it against a timer promise that **rejects** after the limit: `Promise.race([promise, timeout(3000)])` settles with whichever finishes first, so if the timer wins you get a rejection.

```js
const timeout = ms =>
  new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timed out after ${ms} ms`)), ms)
  );

const withTimeout = (promise, ms) => Promise.race([promise, timeout(ms)]);

const slow = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('data'), 5000));
withTimeout(slow, 3000)
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.log(err.message)); // 'Timed out after 3000 ms'
```

Pitfall: `Promise.race` does not **cancel** the losing promise; the slow work keeps running. For `fetch`, prefer the built-in signal, which actually aborts the request:

```js
const res = await fetch('/api/users', { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3000) });
// rejects with a 'TimeoutError' DOMException if it takes longer
```

