back:
JavaScript has no built-in, so check each kind of value explicitly: **falsy values** (`null`, `undefined`, `''`, `0`, `false`, `NaN`), **whitespace-only strings**, **empty arrays and plain objects**, **empty `Map`/`Set`**, and **invalid `Date`s**. Check the type first, then the "emptiness" rule for that type.

```js
const isBlank = value => {
  if (!value) return true; // null, undefined, '', 0, false, NaN
  if (typeof value === 'string') return value.trim() === '';
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.length === 0;
  if (value instanceof Map || value instanceof Set) return value.size === 0;
  if (value instanceof Date) return Number.isNaN(value.getTime());
  if (Object.getPrototypeOf(value) === Object.prototype)
    return Object.keys(value).length === 0;
  return false;
};

isBlank('  \n'); // true
isBlank({}); // true
isBlank(new Date('hello')); // true
isBlank(new Date()); // false
isBlank(new Map([['a', 1]])); // false
```

Pitfall: a generic "no own keys means empty" rule is wrong: a valid `Date`, a non-empty `Map` and a function all have zero own keys. Also decide deliberately whether `0` and `false` are blank: for a "quantity" or "is admin" field they are real answers.

