It throws `TypeError: Class extends value does not have valid prototype property undefined`, because the **`Proxy` constructor has no `prototype` property**, so there is nothing for a subclass to inherit from. Instead, **return a proxy from your class's constructor**: `new MyClass()` then gives you the proxy instead of `this`.

```js
class Tracked {
  constructor(data) {
    Object.assign(this, data);
    return new Proxy(this, {
      set(target, key, value) {
        console.log(`Called with ${key} = ${value}`);
        target[key] = value;
        return true;
      },
    });
  }
}

const user = new Tracked({ name: 'Ada' });
user.email = 'ada@example.com'; // logs 'Called with email = ada@example.com'
user instanceof Tracked; // true
```

Proxies are not prototype-less: `Object.getPrototypeOf(proxy)` is forwarded to the target, which is why `instanceof` still works here. A `return` of an object from a constructor replaces the normal result of `new`, which is what makes this pattern work.

