Check its **prototype**: a plain object created with `{}` or `JSON.parse` has `Object.prototype` as its prototype (or `null` if made with `Object.create(null)`). Arrays, maps, dates and class instances have other prototypes.

```js
const isPlainObject = val => {
  if (val === null || typeof val !== 'object') return false;
  const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(val);
  return proto === Object.prototype || proto === null;
};

isPlainObject({ a: 1 }); // true
isPlainObject(JSON.parse('{"id":1}')); // true
isPlainObject([]); // false
isPlainObject(new Map()); // false
isPlainObject(new Date()); // false
```

A shorter common version is `!!val && typeof val === 'object' && val.constructor === Object`. It works for everyday values but is fooled by an object with its own `constructor` key and rejects `Object.create(null)` objects. Typical use: deciding whether to recurse into a value when deep-merging config or cleaning an API payload.

