Define a **`get` trap**: it runs on every property read with the target and the property name, and whatever it returns is what the caller sees. Transform strings, and pass everything else through unchanged.

```js
const form = { name: 'Ada  ', city: '  Oslo ', age: 36 };

const trimmed = new Proxy(form, {
  get(target, prop) {
    const value = target[prop];
    return typeof value === 'string' ? value.trim() : value;
  },
});

trimmed.name; // 'Ada'
trimmed.city; // 'Oslo'
trimmed.age; // 36
trimmed.email; // undefined
```

Because the trap handles *any* key, it also covers properties added later. You never define a getter per property. The underlying object is not changed (`form.name` is still `'Ada  '`). In everyday code, trimming once when you read the form is simpler. Proxies are worth it when you need the rule applied everywhere automatically.

