ESLint deprecated its **stylistic (formatting) rules** in v8.53 because keeping dozens of layout rules in the core was a big maintenance cost, and dedicated formatters do the job better. Use **Prettier** for formatting, and keep ESLint for code-quality rules (bugs, unsafe patterns). If you want ESLint to enforce style anyway, the rules continue in the community **`@stylistic/eslint-plugin`**.

```js
// eslint.config.js: ESLint for quality, Prettier for layout
import js from '@eslint/js';
import prettier from 'eslint-config-prettier';

export default [
  js.configs.recommended,
  { rules: { eqeqeq: 'error', 'no-unused-vars': 'warn' } },
  prettier,
];
```

The defaults in `js.configs.recommended` are a good start. Add only rules that catch mistakes your team actually makes, rather than copying a huge list.

