Use **`jest.spyOn(object, 'method')`**, then `.mockReturnValue(value)` (or `.mockImplementation(fn)`). The spy replaces the real method so your test is deterministic. **Restore it after each test** with `jest.restoreAllMocks()` (or `restoreMocks: true` in the Jest config) so other tests get the real method back.

```js
afterEach(() => jest.restoreAllMocks());

test('creates an id from the current time', () => {
  jest.spyOn(Date, 'now').mockReturnValue(1_700_000_000_000); // a number, like the real one
  jest.spyOn(Math, 'random').mockReturnValue(0.5);
  expect(makeId()).toBe('1700000000000-5');
});
```

Keep mocked return values the same type as the real ones: `Date.now()` returns a number, so a string mock can hide bugs. For code that uses `new Date()` or timers, `jest.useFakeTimers()` with `jest.setSystemTime(...)` is usually cleaner. Vitest has the same API under `vi.spyOn`.

