A module can have **at most one** `export default`, and the importing file **chooses any name** for it and imports it **without braces**.

```js
// api-client.js
const apiClient = {
  baseUrl: '/api',
  getUsers: () => fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json()),
};
export default apiClient;

// users-page.js
import api from './api-client.js'; // any name works
api.getUsers();
```

Pitfall: because every importer picks its own name, the same thing can end up called `api`, `client` and `apiClient` in different files, which makes searching and refactoring harder. That is why many teams prefer named exports. Also note the path: `'./api-client.js'` is a relative file, while a bare name like `'environment'` would be looked up as an npm package.

