Use **`camelCase`** starting with a lowercase letter, and name functions with a **verb (or verb phrase) that says what they do**: `getUser`, `createOrder`, `formatPrice`, `validateEmail`. Functions that return a boolean usually read as a question: `isValid`, `hasAccess`, `canEdit`.

```js
async function fetchUsers() { /* ... */ }
function formatPrice(cents) { return `$${(cents / 100).toFixed(2)}`; }
const isOverdue = invoice => invoice.dueDate < Date.now();
function handleSubmit(event) { /* event handler */ }
```

Names are case-sensitive (`getUser` and `getuser` are different functions). Common prefixes: `get`/`fetch` (read), `create`/`update`/`delete` (write), `to`/`format` (convert), and `handle` or `on` for event handlers. Class methods follow the same rules.

