Use **`UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`** for fixed, hard-coded values known before the program runs, usually at the top of a module: configuration and "magic numbers" such as `MAX_RETRIES` or `API_BASE_URL`. Use **`camelCase`** for a `const` whose value is computed at runtime, even though it is never reassigned.

```js
const MAX_UPLOAD_MB = 10;
const API_BASE_URL = '/api/v1';

async function loadUser(id) {
  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/users/${id}`); // camelCase: runtime value
  const user = await response.json();
  return user;
}
```

`const` only stops the variable from being *reassigned*. It does not make the value immutable, so `const settings = {}` can still be changed with `settings.theme = 'dark'`. The naming signals intent to readers; the language doesn't enforce it.

