back:
`??=` assigns **only if the current value is `null` or `undefined`**. `||=` assigns if the current value is **any falsy value**, so it also overwrites a deliberate `0`, `''` or `false`.

```js
const a = { pageSize: 0 };
a.pageSize ??= 20;
a.pageSize; // 0 (kept: 0 is not nullish)

const b = { pageSize: 0 };
b.pageSize ||= 20;
b.pageSize; // 20 (0 was treated as "missing")

const c = {};
c.pageSize ??= 20;
c.pageSize; // 20
```

It is shorthand for `x ?? (x = value)`: if the value is already set, **no assignment happens at all**. Typical use: filling in defaults on an options object or a request's query parameters. Logical assignment (`??=`, `||=`, `&&=`) is ES2021 and supported everywhere modern.

