Converting from HSL to HSB is a matter of **scaling the saturation and lightness** values to match the brightness value. This can be done using [a simple formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HSL_and_HSV#HSL_to_HSV), where the saturation is multiplied by the minimum of the lightness and 1 minus the lightness, and the lightness is added to the result.

The range of all **input parameters** is H: [0, 360], S: [0, 100], L: [0, 100], while the range of all **output values** is H: [0, 360], S: [0, 100], B: [0, 100].

```js
const hslToHsb = (h, s, l) => {
  const b = l + (s / 100) * Math.min(l, 100 - l);
  s = b === 0 ? 0 : 2 * (1 - l / b) * 100;
  return [h, s, b];
};

hslToHsb(13, 100, 11); // [13, 100, 22]
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/rgb-hex-hsl-hsb-color-format-conversion.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
The **hue stays the same**. Brightness is **B = L + S × min(L, 100 − L)** (with S as a 0–1 fraction). The new saturation is **S = 2 × (1 − L / B)** × 100, or 0 when B is 0 (black).

```js
// h: 0-360, s and l: 0-100 → [h, s, b] with s and b: 0-100
const hslToHsb = (h, s, l) => {
  const b = l + (s / 100) * Math.min(l, 100 - l);
  s = b === 0 ? 0 : 2 * (1 - l / b) * 100;
  return [h, s, b];
};

hslToHsb(13, 100, 11); // [13, 100, 22]
hslToHsb(0, 0, 100); // [0, 0, 100] (white)
```

Where you meet this: design tools such as Figma and Photoshop show HSB/HSV pickers, while CSS understands `hsl()` but has no `hsb()` function. So converting between the two helps when turning a designer's HSB values into CSS.

