In the past, swapping the values of two variables in JavaScript required an intermediate variable to store one of the values while swapping, which would result in something similar to this:

```js
let a = 10;
let b = 20;

let tmp;
tmp = a;
a = b;
b = tmp;
```

While this approach still works, there are more elegant and less verbose options available to us nowadays. For example, JavaScript ES6 introduced **destructuring assignments**, allowing individual array items to be assigned to variables in a single statement. Here's what that looks like:

```js
const [x, y] = [1, 2];
```

Destructuring assignments are extremely useful in a handful of situations, including swapping two variables. To accomplish this, we can create an array from the two variables, then use a destructuring assignment to reassign them to each other:

```js
let a = 10;
let b = 20;

[a , b] = [b, a];
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/swap-two-variables.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use **array destructuring assignment**: `[a, b] = [b, a];`. The right side builds a temporary array of the current values, and the left side assigns them back in swapped order, with no `tmp` variable.

```js
let a = 10;
let b = 20;
[a, b] = [b, a];
a; // 20
b; // 10

// Swap two items, e.g. moving a list row up
const items = ['x', 'y', 'z'];
[items[0], items[1]] = [items[1], items[0]];
items; // ['y', 'x', 'z']
```

Gotcha: if the previous line has **no semicolon**, JavaScript joins the lines. `let b = 20` followed by `[a, b] = [b, a]` parses as `let b = 20[a, b] = [b, a]`, which throws `ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization`. Keep semicolons, or start the line with `;` in no-semicolon code styles.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/swap-two-variables.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

`"files"` is an allowlist of what goes into the published package (here only `dist/`), `"type": "module"` makes `.js` files ES modules, and `"exports"` defines the import paths consumers may use (anything not listed can't be deep-imported). Then run `npm login` once and `npm publish`.

```json
{
  "name": "readex",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "files": ["dist"],
  "exports": {
    ".": { "import": "./dist/readex.js" }
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "vite build",
    "prepublishOnly": "npm run build"
  }
}
```

Before publishing, run `npm pack --dry-run` to list exactly which files would be uploaded. The `prepublishOnly` script rebuilds `dist/` first, so you never publish a stale or missing build. (`"module"` is an older hint read only by bundlers; Node and modern bundlers use `"exports"`.)

