A subclass *is* a real `RegExp`, so every instance works anywhere a regex is expected (`.test()`, `str.match()`, `str.replace()`) and already exposes its pattern text as `.source`, so no custom `.value` is needed.

```js
class Segment extends RegExp {
  constructor(expression) {
    // accept a string, a RegExp, or another Segment
    super(expression instanceof RegExp ? expression.source : expression);
  }
}

const s = new Segment(new Segment('abc'));
s.test('xabcx');      // true
s instanceof RegExp;  // true
'abc'.match(s)[0];    // 'abc'
```

Passing `.source` deliberately drops flags: `new Segment(/abc/i).flags` is `''`. Testing gotcha: Vitest's `toMatch` wants the *string* as the received value (`expect('abc').toMatch(/b/)`); `expect(regex).toMatch('abc')` throws a `TypeError`. Use `expect(regex.test('abc')).toBe(true)` or a custom matcher.

