back:
`.source` returns a RegExp's pattern text without the slashes or flags (`/a+b/gi.source` is `'a+b'`). So the constructor stores strings as-is, RegExps via `.source`, and existing wrappers via their stored value; every input ends up as plain text that can be joined into bigger patterns.

```js
class Segment {
  constructor(expression) {
    if (expression instanceof Segment) this.value = expression.value;
    else if (expression instanceof RegExp) this.value = expression.source;
    else if (typeof expression === 'string') this.value = expression;
    else throw new TypeError('Expected a string, RegExp, or Segment');
  }
}

new Segment(/a+b/gi).value;          // 'a+b'
new Segment(new Segment('x')).value; // 'x'
```

End with a `throw`: with only separate `if`s, an unexpected input (a number, `undefined`) silently leaves `value` as `undefined` and fails much later.

