back:
Call `expect.extend({ name(received, ...args) { ... } })` and return `{ pass, message }`. Vitest inverts the result for `.not` by itself; `this.isNot` only lets you word the failure message correctly.

```js
import { expect } from 'vitest';

expect.extend({
  toMatchString(regex, string) {
    const pass = regex.test(string);
    return {
      pass,
      message: () =>
        `expected ${regex}${this.isNot ? ' not' : ''} to match "${string}"`,
    };
  },
});

expect(/^[\da-f]$/).toMatchString('a');
expect(/^[\da-f]$/).not.toMatchString('g');
```

Why it's needed: the built-in `toMatch` expects the *string* as the received value (`expect('abc').toMatch(/b/)`), and `expect(/b/).toMatch('abc')` throws a `TypeError`. Register matchers once in a `setupFiles` file instead of importing them in every test.

