back:
Put the required special arguments first, before the main input. Then a missing one is obvious at the call site, every function in the library follows one consistent shape, and readers see the arguments that change behavior before the (often long) main argument.

```js
// Hard to scan: the settings hide after a long pattern
const before = repeat(anythingFrom(['a', 'z']), 3, true);

// Special arguments first
const after = repeat(3, true, anythingFrom(['a', 'z']));
```

This still leaves a problem: `3, true` does not say what it means. A common next step is an options object with named fields, e.g. `repeat({ times: 3, lazy: true }, pattern)`, so each value is labelled at the call site.

