back:
Use the established term because users already know it: they can guess the API from their existing knowledge and can search docs for it. Invented "friendly" names force them to learn a second vocabulary and can mislead: `\b` is a zero-width position between a word and non-word character, not a separator character.

```js
// Good: standard regex vocabulary
const wordBoundary = '\\b';
const whitespaceCharacter = '\\s';
const zeroOrOne = '?';
const oneOrMoreLazy = '+?';

// Bad: invented or vague names
const wordSeparator = '\\b';
const space = '\\s';   // \s also matches tabs and newlines
const maybe = '?';
```

The same applies in app code: name things with the words your team and domain already use (`invoice`, `refund`), not new synonyms.

