Variables declared with `var` are **function scoped**, in contrast to variables declared with `let` or `const` which are **block scoped**.

```js
const scopeExample = () => {
  var a = 'var';
  let b = 'let';
  console.log(a, b); // 'var', 'let'

  {
    var c = 'var';
    let d = 'let';
    console.log(c, d); // 'var', 'let'
  }

  console.log(c); // 'var'
  console.log(d); // Throws a ReferenceError
};
```

If you want to learn more, you can read [the article covering JavaScript variables and scopes in more depth](https://www.30secondsofcode.org/js/s/variable-scope).

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/js/s/var-let-const.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Only with `var`. `var` is function-scoped: it belongs to the whole enclosing function (or the global scope), so it is still visible after the block. `let` and `const` are block-scoped: outside the `{ }` they don't exist, and reading them throws a `ReferenceError`.

```js
const scopeExample = () => {
  {
    var c = 'var';
    let d = 'let';
  }

  console.log(c); // 'var'
  console.log(d); // ReferenceError: d is not defined
};
```

Real-world impact: `for (var i = 0; ...)` leaks `i` outside the loop and shares one `i` across callbacks created in the loop; `for (let i = 0; ...)` gives each iteration its own `i`.

