back:
Mutation changes an object that other code may also hold, so a change in one place shows up unexpectedly elsewhere, and React won't re-render when you mutate state in place. Return a new object instead: `const birthday = user => ({ ...user, age: user.age + 1 });`. Then the function is pure (same input, same output, no side effects) and easy to test.

```js
const user = { name: 'Alice', age: 25 };

// Mutating: changes the caller's object
const birthdayMut = u => { u.age++; };

// Pure: returns a copy with the change
const birthday = u => ({ ...u, age: u.age + 1 });

const older = birthday(user);
user.age;  // 25
older.age; // 26
```

Two gotchas: an arrow function returning an object literal needs parentheses `({ ... })`, otherwise the braces are read as a function body. And spread is a shallow copy; nested objects are still shared (use `structuredClone` for a deep copy).

