back:
Pull each condition into a small, well-named predicate function and use an early return (a "guard clause") to exit when the preconditions fail. The main function then reads like a sentence and each predicate can be tested separately.

```js
// Before: 4 levels deep
const processOrder = order => {
  if (order.paid) {
    if (order.items.length > 0) {
      for (const item of order.items) {
        if (item.active) {
          if (item.value > 10) { /* ... */ }
        }
      }
    }
  }
};

// After
const isProcessable = order => order.paid && order.items.length > 0;
const isActiveAndValuable = item => item.active && item.value > 10;

const processOrder = order => {
  if (!isProcessable(order)) return; // guard clause
  for (const item of order.items.filter(isActiveAndValuable)) {
    /* ... */
  }
};
```

Deep nesting makes readers track every open condition at once; predicates plus guard clauses keep each level simple.

