To create a **fill animation** on hover, set a `color` and `background` and use an appropriate `transition` to animate changes to the element. Use the `:hover` pseudo-class to change the `background` and `color` of the element when the user hovers over it.

```css
.button-fill {
  background: #fff;
  color: #000;
  transition: all 0.3s ease-in-out;
}

.button-fill:hover {
  background: #000;
  color: #fff;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/button-hover-transitions.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
A `transition` on the base rule applies in both directions: the colors animate when hover starts and animate back when it ends. If you put it only inside `:hover`, the transition disappears the moment hover ends, so the colors snap back instantly.

```css
.button-fill {
  background: #fff;
  color: #000;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease-in-out, color 0.3s ease-in-out;
}

.button-fill:hover,
.button-fill:focus-visible {
  background: #000;
  color: #fff;
}
```

Name the properties instead of using `transition: all`. `all` also animates things you did not intend, such as a width change from a media query. Pair `:hover` with `:focus-visible` so keyboard users get the same feedback, and check that both color states have enough contrast.

