For a **swing effect**, you can create an animation, using `@keyframes` and an appropriate `transition` to animate changes to the element. Use the `:hover` pseudo-class to apply an `animation` that uses `transform` to make the element swing.

```css
.button-swing {
  transition: all 0.2s ease-in-out;
}

.button-swing:hover {
  animation: swing 1s ease;
  animation-iteration-count: 1;
}

@keyframes swing {
  15% {
    transform: translateX(5px);
  }
  30% {
    transform: translateX(-5px);
  }
  50% {
    transform: translateX(3px);
  }
  65% {
    transform: translateX(-3px);
  }
  80% {
    transform: translateX(2px);
  }
  100% {
    transform: translateX(0);
  }
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/button-hover-transitions.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
A transition can only animate between two states (from the normal value to the hover value). A wiggle needs several intermediate positions (right, left, smaller right…), so you define them as `@keyframes` and run that animation once when `:hover` starts to apply.

```css
.button-swing:hover {
  animation: swing 1s ease 1;   /* 1 = play once */
}

@keyframes swing {
  15% { transform: translateX(5px); }
  30% { transform: translateX(-5px); }
  50% { transform: translateX(3px); }
  65% { transform: translateX(-3px); }
  80% { transform: translateX(2px); }
  100% { transform: translateX(0); }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .button-swing:hover { animation: none; }
}
```

The animation restarts every time the pointer re-enters, because `:hover` stops matching and then matches again. It uses `transform`, which does not trigger layout, so it is cheap to animate. A `transition` on the base rule adds nothing here.

