```css
.perspective-card {
  transform: perspective(1500px) rotateY(15deg);
  transition: transform 1s ease 0s;
}

.perspective-card:hover {
  transform: perspective(3000px) rotateY(5deg);
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/card-hover-effects.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`perspective()` sets how far the viewer is from the element. Without it, `rotateY()` just looks like horizontal squashing with no depth; smaller values give stronger depth. The hover rule must repeat it because a new `transform` value replaces the whole function list. Leave `perspective()` out and the hover state renders flat, with no depth.

```css
.perspective-card {
  transform: perspective(1500px) rotateY(15deg);
  transition: transform 1s ease;
}

.perspective-card:hover {
  transform: perspective(3000px) rotateY(5deg);
}
```

When both lists have the same functions in the same order, the browser interpolates them function by function; otherwise it falls back to matrix interpolation, which can take an odd path. The `perspective` property (on a parent) is the alternative when several children should share one 3D viewpoint.

