Cutout effects are a great way to add visual interest to your website. They can be used to create unique layouts, highlight important content, or simply make your website more visually appealing. One common way to create a cutout effect is to use an image cutout, where an image is displayed inside a container with a cutout shape.

To create this effect, you'll need a **container element** with a colored `background` and a **card element** containing the image you want to display. You can then use the `::before` pseudo-element to add a `border` around the image, matching the container element's `background` and creating the illusion of a cutout in the card.

```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="card">
    <figure>
      <img src="/path/to/img.jpg"/>
    </figure>
    <p class="content">Card content</p>
  </div>
</div>
```

```css
.container {
  background: #f3f1fe;
}

.card {
  background: #fff;
  box-shadow: 0 0 5px -2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

.card figure {
  width: 120px;
  height: 120px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  margin-top: -60px;
  position: relative;
}

.card figure::before {
  content: "";
  border-radius: inherit;
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  border: 1rem solid #f3f1fe;
  box-shadow: 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

.card figure img {
  border-radius: inherit;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/card-image-cutout.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
It is a fake: a pseudo-element draws a thick ring around the avatar in the same color as the page background behind the card, so the ring looks like a hole in the card. It only works when that background is one solid, known color. On a gradient, image, or theme change, the ring shows up as a visible band.

```css
.card figure {
  position: relative;
  width: 120px;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: 50%;
  margin-top: -60px;           /* pull the avatar half out of the card */
}

.card figure::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -1rem;                /* ring extends 1rem beyond the image */
  border-radius: inherit;
  border: 1rem solid var(--page-bg);   /* same color as the page */
}

.card figure img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  border-radius: inherit;
  object-fit: cover;
}
```

Using a custom property such as `--page-bg` for the ring keeps it in sync with theme changes. For a real transparent hole, use `mask` with a `radial-gradient()` instead.

