Using **flexbox** to vertically and horizontally center content is usually the **preferred method**. All it takes is three lines of code in the container element to set `display: flex` and then center the child element vertically and horizontally using `align-items: center` and `justify-content: center` respectively.

```html
<div class="flexbox-centering">
  <div class="content">Content</div>
</div>
```

```css
.flexbox-centering {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/centering.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Make the container a flex container and set `justify-content: center` (main axis) and `align-items: center` (cross axis). You can also use grid with one line: `place-items: center`.

```css
.center-flex {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 200px;   /* vertical centering needs spare height */
}

.center-grid {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
}
```

A common mistake: the container is only as tall as its content, so vertical centering appears to "do nothing". Give it a height or `min-height`. `margin: auto` on a flex or grid child also centers it.

