Transform centering uses, as the name implies, **CSS transforms** to center an element. It depends on the container element having a `position: relative`, allowing the child element to utilize `position: absolute` to position itself. Then `left: 50%` and `top: 50%` are used to **offset** the child element and `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` to **negate** its position.

```html
<div class="transform-centering">
  <div class="content">Content</div>
</div>
```

```css
.transform-centering {
  position: relative;
}

.transform-centering > .content {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/centering.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`top/left: 50%` moves the element's top-left corner to the parent's center. Percentages in `translate()` refer to the element's own size, so `translate(-50%, -50%)` pulls it back by half its own width and height and centers it exactly, even when its size is unknown.

```css
.parent { position: relative; }

.parent > .overlay {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

This is still useful for absolutely positioned overlays (badges, modal boxes, loaders) whose size depends on content. For normal layout, prefer flex or grid centering. If the element already has another `transform` (e.g. an animation), the two will overwrite each other. Use the standalone `translate: -50% -50%` property to avoid that.

