Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/circular-progress-bar.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Custom properties chain the math: radius → circumference → dash length. Then `stroke-dasharray: var(--dash) calc(var(--circumference) - var(--dash))` draws exactly that much of the ring. Changing or animating `--progress` is all you need to update the bar.

```css
.circular-progress {
  --size: 250px;
  --stroke-width: 20px;
  --radius: calc((var(--size) - var(--stroke-width)) / 2);
  --circumference: calc(2 * pi * var(--radius));
  --dash: calc(var(--progress) * var(--circumference) / 100);
}

.circular-progress circle.fg {
  transform: rotate(-90deg);
  transform-origin: center;
  stroke-dasharray: var(--dash) calc(var(--circumference) - var(--dash));
  stroke-linecap: round;
}
```

`--progress` must be registered with `@property` (as `<number>`) if you want to animate it. From JS, `el.style.setProperty('--progress', 42)` updates the bar. Also keep `aria-valuenow` in sync so screen readers get the value.

