When using the old-school `float` property to build layouts, you may encounter issues with elements not clearing their floated children. This can lead to unexpected **layout problems**, such as elements overlapping or not aligning properly.

To fix this issue, the **clearfix** hack was developed, which allows an element to self-clear its children without the need for additional non-semantic markup.

What you need to do is to create a pseudo-element with `content: ''` and apply `clear: both` to it. This will make the element clear past both left and right floats, ensuring that the layout behaves as expected.

For this technique to work properly, make sure there are **no non-floating children** in the container and that there are no tall floats before the container with the clearfix but in the same formatting context (e.g. floated columns).

> [!NOTE]
>
> This is only useful if you are using `float` to build layouts. Consider using a more modern approach, such as the flexbox or grid layout.

```html
<div class="clearfix">
  <div class="floated">float a</div>
  <div class="floated">float b</div>
  <div class="floated">float c</div>
</div>
```

```css
.clearfix::after {
  content: '';
  display: block;
  clear: both;
}

.floated {
  float: left;
  padding: 4px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/clearfix.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Floats are taken out of normal flow, so the parent doesn't grow to contain them. The classic clearfix adds an empty `::after` block that clears both floats and forces the parent to extend below them. The modern fix is `display: flow-root`. It makes the parent a new block formatting context, and a block formatting context always contains its floats.

```css
/* Legacy clearfix */
.clearfix::after {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  clear: both;
}

/* Modern */
.container { display: flow-root; }
```

Today, use flexbox or grid for layout and keep `float` for its original job: wrapping text around an image. You will still see clearfix in older codebases and CSS frameworks.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/clearfix.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Look for convention issues that tools can't judge: class naming that follows the team's scheme (e.g. BEM or component-scoped names), hard-coded colors or spacing that should use existing design tokens or custom properties (`var(--space-2)` instead of `13px`), and missing comments on non-obvious values (magic numbers, browser workarounds).

```css
/* Flag in review */
.Card_title2 { margin-top: 13px; color: #3a3a3a; }

/* Prefer */
.card__title { margin-block-start: var(--space-3); color: var(--text-muted); }
```

If the project has no CSS linter (e.g. Stylelint) and formatter (e.g. Prettier), suggest adding them, so reviews stop spending time on indentation and property order.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/code-reviews.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Flag things with visible cost: animating layout properties (`width`, `height`, `top`, `margin`) instead of `transform`/`opacity`, `will-change` scattered everywhere, huge duplicated rule blocks, and `@import` chains that delay rendering. Don't bother with micro-optimizing selector speed: modern engines match selectors fast enough that it rarely matters.

```css
/* Flag: animates layout every frame */
.toast { transition: top 0.3s; }

/* Prefer: compositor-friendly */
.toast { transition: transform 0.3s; }
```

Duplication and long override chains are the most common real problems. They make the CSS bigger and harder to change. When in doubt, ask for a DevTools Performance profile rather than guessing.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/code-reviews.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Declarations that no longer do anything after the author's experiments: flex or grid properties on elements that aren't flex or grid containers or items, `z-index` on non-positioned elements, `top/left` without `position`, overridden duplicates, and rules for classes that no longer exist in the markup.

```css
.sidebar {
  display: block;
  justify-content: space-between; /* no effect: not a flex/grid container */
  z-index: 10;                    /* no effect: not positioned */
  margin: 0;
  margin: 0 auto;                 /* first margin is dead */
}
```

Dead declarations cause trouble later: someone changes `display` to `flex`, and the forgotten `justify-content` suddenly takes effect. Firefox and Chrome DevTools gray out or annotate many inactive properties, which makes them quicker to spot.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/code-reviews.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Keep specificity as low as possible and selectors neither too generic nor too complex. Flag IDs, long descendant chains, qualifying classes with element names, and `!important`. These force the next change to use an even stronger selector. Also flag bare element selectors that unintentionally style the whole app.

```css
/* Flag */
#main .content div.card > ul li a.link { color: red !important; }
a { color: red; }   /* hits every link in the app */

/* Prefer */
.card-link { color: var(--danger); }
```

`:where()` has zero specificity, which is handy for base styles meant to be easy to override. Cascade layers (`@layer`) let you order groups of styles without raising specificity.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/code-reviews.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

