As you may be aware, the `height` property cannot be transitioned from `0` to `auto` directly. Instead, we usually rely on `max-height` to transition an element's height from `0` to a known value. However, when **the height of the element is unknown**, we can use a combination of JavaScript and CSS variables to transition the height from `0` to `auto`.

The trick here is to leverage [CSS custom properties](https://www.30secondsofcode.org/css/s/variables) to alter the `max-height`. Then, using JavaScript, we can change the value of the custom property to the actual **height of the element's content**.

So, how do we do that, exactly? Provided we've defined a `--max-height` CSS variable with, we can use `Element.scrollHeight` and `CSSStyleDeclaration.setProperty()` to set the value of the custom property to the **current height of the element**. This way, we can essentially transition the height of the element from `0` to `auto`.

In order to complete the effect and apply it on hover, we'll use the `:hover` pseudo-class to change the `max-height` to the value of the `--max-height` variable set by JavaScript. We'll also add a `transition` for the `max-height` property to **animate the height change**.

And voilà! You've got yourself a content-aware height transition.

> [!CAUTION]
>
> This trick causes a **reflow on each animation frame**, which will be laggy if there are a large number of elements beneath the element that is transitioning in height.

```html
<div class="trigger">
  Hover me to see a height transition.
  <div class="el">Additional content</div>
</div>
```

```css
.el {
  transition: max-height 0.3s;
  overflow: hidden;
  max-height: 0;
}

.trigger:hover > .el {
  max-height: var(--max-height);
}
```

```js
const el = document.querySelector('.el');
const height = el.scrollHeight;

el.style.setProperty('--max-height', height + 'px');
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/content-aware-height-transition.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Classic trick: animate `max-height` instead, and have JavaScript set it to the element's measured `scrollHeight` through a custom property, so the target is the real content height rather than a guessed large number.

```css
.panel {
  overflow: hidden;
  max-height: 0;
  transition: max-height 0.3s;
}
.trigger:is(:hover, :focus-within) > .panel {
  max-height: var(--max-height);
}
```

```js
const panel = document.querySelector('.panel');
panel.style.setProperty('--max-height', panel.scrollHeight + 'px');
```

Re-measure whenever the content changes. Modern options need no JavaScript. A grid wrapper can transition `grid-template-rows` from `0fr` to `1fr` (with `overflow: hidden` on the child); this works in all modern browsers. `interpolate-size: allow-keywords` allows animating to `height: auto` directly, but browser support is still limited. Any height animation re-runs layout on every frame, so keep it to small panels.

---
