Scrollbar styling is one of the longest-awaited features in CSS. While it's not officially supported, there are still ways to style scrollbars in certain browsers.

> [!CAUTION]
>
> Scrollbar styling doesn't appear to be on any standards track. This technique only works on **WebKit-based browsers** (Chrome, Edge, Safari).

In general, you can use the `::-webkit-scrollbar` pseudo-element to style the **scrollbar element**, `::-webkit-scrollbar-track` to style the **scrollbar track** (the background of the scrollbar), and `::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb` to style the **scrollbar thumb** (the draggable element).

Putting everything together, you can create a style for the scrollbar like this:

```css
.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar {
  width: 8px;
}

.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
  background: #4a7856;
  border-radius: 12px;
}

.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: #70bceb;
  border-radius: 12px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/custom-scrollbar.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use the standard properties: `scrollbar-color: <thumb> <track>` and `scrollbar-width: auto | thin | none` (defined in the CSS Scrollbars spec). They work in Firefox and in Chrome/Edge 121+. The non-standard `::-webkit-scrollbar` pseudo-elements allow finer control (exact width, rounded thumb) and are now only a fallback for browsers without the standard properties.

```css
.custom-scrollbar {
  scrollbar-color: #70bceb #4a7856;  /* thumb, track */
  scrollbar-width: thin;
}

/* Fallback for browsers without the standard properties */
.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; }
.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: #4a7856; }
.custom-scrollbar::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: #70bceb; border-radius: 12px; }
```

In Chromium, once `scrollbar-color` or `scrollbar-width` is set, the `::-webkit-scrollbar` rules are ignored. Keep enough contrast between thumb and track so users can still see the scroll position.

