In order to create a list with **floating headings for each section**, you can use `overflow-y: auto` to allow the list container to overflow vertically. Then, use `display: grid` on the inner container (`<dl>`) to create a layout with two columns.

Set headings (`<dt>`) to `grid-column: 1` and content (`<dd>`) to `grid-column: 2`. Finally, apply `position: sticky` and `top: 0.5rem` to headings to create a floating effect.

```css
.floating-stack {
  overflow-y: auto;
}

.floating-stack > dl {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 1fr;
  align-items: center;
}

.floating-stack dt {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0.5rem;
  grid-column: 1;
}

.floating-stack dd {
  grid-column: 2;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/floating-or-sticky-list-titles.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Wrap each letter's `<dt>` and its `<dd>`s in a `<div>` (allowed inside `<dl>`), make each group a two-column grid (letter in column 1, names in column 2) inside a scroll container, and give each `<dt>` `position: sticky; top: 0.5rem`. Each letter sticks near the top while its group is in view, then scrolls away with the group.

```css
.floating-stack {
  height: 320px;          /* the scroll container needs a limited height */
  overflow-y: auto;
}
.floating-stack dl > div {       /* one group per letter */
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 1fr;
}
.floating-stack dt {
  grid-column: 1;
  position: sticky;
  top: 0.5rem;
}
.floating-stack dd {
  grid-column: 2;
  margin: 0;
}
```

A sticky element only sticks within its parent box. If the whole list is one grid, every `<dt>` can stick until the end of the list, so the letters pile up on top of each other. One wrapper per section limits each letter to its own group.

