Most often, no threshold is set: sticky needs `top` (or `bottom`/`left`/`right`), and without one it acts like `relative`. Other common causes: an ancestor between the heading and the intended scroller has `overflow: hidden/auto`, which becomes the sticky scope instead; the scroll container has no limited height, so it never scrolls; or the heading's parent is only as tall as the heading, so it has no room to stick.

```css
.sticky-stack {
  max-height: 400px;
  overflow-y: auto;
}
.sticky-stack dt {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;            /* required */
  background: #fff;  /* so rows don't show through */
}
```

For debugging, DevTools can show you which ancestor is the scroll container. Give sticky headers a background, or the content will be visible behind them as it scrolls.

