`clamp(min, preferred, max)` uses the preferred value but never goes below min or above max. Here the text is 1rem on narrow screens and 3rem on wide ones, and in between it scales with viewport width: with a 16px root, `8vw − 2rem` equals 16px at a 600px viewport and 48px at 1000px.

```css
h1 {
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 8vw - 2rem, 3rem);
}
```

Keep `rem` in both bounds and in the preferred value. Text sized only in `vw` doesn't grow when the user zooms or raises their default font size, which fails the WCAG requirement that text can be resized to 200%. A `rem` term keeps part of the size tied to the user's settings. Fluid sizes suit large headings; body text is usually better at a fixed `rem` size.

