Flexbox: make `body` a column flex container at least as tall as the viewport, and give `main` `flex: 1` so it absorbs the extra height and pushes the footer down. Grid does it on the container alone with `grid-template-rows`. Pick grid when you already describe the page as rows (header / main / footer) or will add areas such as a sidebar.

```css
/* Flexbox */
body {
  min-height: 100dvh;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}
main { flex: 1; }

/* Grid */
body {
  min-height: 100dvh;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;  /* header, main, footer */
}
```

Both are correct. The flex version doesn't care how many other children `body` has, while the grid row list must match the actual children.

