I remember a time when gradients were tricky to use for backgrounds, let alone for styling text. But, with modern CSS, it's pretty easy to create gradient text with only a few lines of code, at least on WebKit browsers (Chrome, Edge, Safari).

Styling text with gradients starts the same as creating a **gradient for the background**. You use the `linear-gradient()` function to define the gradient colors and direction.

Then, you have to **apply it to the text** using the `-webkit-text-fill-color` property with a value of `transparent`. Finally, **clip the background with the text** using the `background-clip` property with a value of `text` and you're done!

```css
.gradient-text {
  background: linear-gradient(to right, #009FFF, #ec2F4B);
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
  background-clip: text;
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/gradient-text.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Paint the gradient as the element's background, clip that background to the shape of the glyphs with `background-clip: text`, and make the text itself transparent so the clipped background shows through.

```css
.gradient-text {
  background: linear-gradient(to right, #009fff, #ec2f4b);
  background-clip: text;
  color: transparent;
}
```

If clipping were unsupported, `color: transparent` would make the text invisible; for old browsers, wrap the rule in `@supports (background-clip: text)` and keep a solid `color` outside it. The unprefixed `background-clip: text` works in current Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari; `-webkit-background-clip` / `-webkit-text-fill-color` only matter for older browsers. Check contrast against the page at both ends of the gradient, and remember selected text and Windows high-contrast mode may render differently.

