A more traditional way to achieve a similar effect is by using a `linear-gradient()` background. This method is less flexible than `backdrop-filter`, but it works well in most cases.

Depending on your needs, you can adjust the gradient's colors, direction, and opacity to create different overlay effects.

```html
<figure>
  <img src="/path/to/img.jpg">
  <figcaption>Text</figcaption>
</figure>
```

```css
figure {
  position: relative;
}

figcaption {
  position: absolute;
  color: #fff;
  background: linear-gradient(0deg, #00000088 30%, #ffffff44 100%);
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/image-text-overlay.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Photos have unpredictable light and dark areas, so white text can become unreadable. A gradient that gets darker toward the text guarantees contrast wherever the text sits, while the rest of the image stays visible. Pin the caption with `position: absolute` and `inset` values inside a `position: relative` figure.

```css
figure { position: relative; margin: 0; }
figure img { display: block; width: 100%; }

figcaption {
  position: absolute;
  inset: auto 0 0 0;          /* bottom edge, full width */
  padding: 2rem 1rem 1rem;
  color: #fff;
  background: linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.75), transparent);
}
```

This works in every browser and is cheap to render. Adjust the stop colors and direction to taste, and check contrast over the brightest image you expect.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/image-text-overlay.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

previous.back:
The most modern technique to create a text overlay is by using the `backdrop-filter` property. This property applies a filter to the backdrop of an element, which is the area behind the element itself.

For an overlay effect, you can use the `blur()` and `brightness()` functions, which apply a blur and adjust the brightness of the backdrop respectively.

```html
<figure>
  <figcaption>Text</figcaption>
  <img src="/path/to/img.jpg">
</figure>
```

```css
figure {
  position: relative;
}

figcaption {
  position: absolute;
  color: #fff;
  backdrop-filter: blur(14px) brightness(75%);
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/image-text-overlay.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`backdrop-filter` blurs and darkens whatever is behind the element (here, the part of the image under the caption), giving a frosted-glass panel that keeps text readable. `filter` applies the effect to the element itself and its content, so it would blur the caption text instead.

```css
figure { position: relative; margin: 0; }

figcaption {
  position: absolute;
  inset: auto 0 0 0;
  padding: 1rem;
  color: #fff;
  background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);   /* fallback tint */
  backdrop-filter: blur(14px) brightness(75%);
}
```

Only areas under a transparent or semi-transparent background are affected. Keep a tinted background as a fallback so text stays readable if the filter is unsupported or disabled. Heavy blurs over large areas cost GPU time, especially on phones.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/image-text-overlay.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

previous.back:
Ever wanted to create an input with a visual, **non-editable prefix**? HTML, CSS and some ingenuity can help you with that!

All you really need is **two elements**: a `<span>` for the prefix and an `<input>` for the actual input field. By styling them accordingly, you can create a visually appealing input box that looks like it has a prefix.

For starters, you can create a **container element** with `display: flex` to align the prefix and the input field. You can then remove the border and outline from the `<input>` field. Apply them to the parent element instead to make it look like an input box.

Finally, you can use the `:focus-within` pseudo-class selector to style the parent element accordingly, when the user interacts with the `<input>` field.

```html
<div class="input-box">
  <span class="prefix">+30</span>
  <input type="tel" placeholder="210 123 4567"/>
</div>
```

```css
.input-box {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  border: 1px solid #a0a0a0;
  border-radius: 4px;
}

.input-box input {
  border: none;
  outline: none;
}

.input-box:focus-within {
  border-color: #4f7df3;
}
```

> [!CAUTION]
>
> Make sure to include your prefix when **submitting the form**. You can use JavaScript to append it to the input value before sending it to the server.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/input-with-prefix.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Put a `<span>` with the prefix and the `<input>` in a flex container, move the border from the input to the container, and use `:focus-within` on the container to draw the focus ring whenever the input is focused. The prefix stays static text that users can't edit or delete.

```html
<label for="phone">Phone</label>
<div class="input-box">
  <span class="prefix">+30</span>
  <input id="phone" type="tel" placeholder="210 123 4567" />
</div>
```

```css
.input-box {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  border: 1px solid #a0a0a0;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
.input-box input { border: none; outline: none; flex: 1; }
.input-box:focus-within {
  outline: 2px solid #4f7df3;   /* replaces the input's removed outline */
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

Only remove the input's `outline` if the container shows a visible focus style instead. The prefix is not part of the input's value: add it when submitting (or store the country code in a separate field) so the server gets the full number.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/input-with-prefix.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

