You can also use `inset` with custom values to position an element in a specific way, leveraging the `auto` keyword to essentially skip some sides. For example, if you want to position an element `10px` from the `top` and `50px` from the `left`, you can skip the two values in the middle.

```html
<div class="box"></div>
```

```css
.box {
  position: absolute;
  /* top: 10px, left: 50px */
  inset: 10px auto auto 50px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
It sets `top: 10px` and `left: 50px`; `right` and `bottom` stay `auto`. `inset` follows the same clockwise order as `margin`/`padding` (top, right, bottom, left), and `auto` means "not constrained on this side", so the box keeps its own size instead of being stretched.

```css
.badge {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 10px auto auto 50px;   /* top right bottom left */
}

/* Same as */
.badge { position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 50px; }
```

Setting opposite sides (both `left` and `right`) stretches an absolutely positioned element without an explicit width. That is why the unused sides must be `auto`, not `0`.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

previous.back:
To center an element within its parent, you can use one or more values of the `inset` property to position it. If, for example, the element is half the size of its parent, you can use `inset: 25%` to center it.

```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child"></div>
</div>
```

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  /* Center the child */
  /* top: 25%, right: 25%, bottom: 25%, left: 25% */
  inset: 25%;
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Set `inset: 0` so all four offsets are 0, give the element an explicit size, and add `margin: auto`. The auto margins absorb the leftover space equally on each side, which centers the box horizontally and vertically.

```css
.parent { position: relative; height: 200px; }

.child {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  margin: auto;        /* splits leftover space evenly */
}
```

This needs a known width and height (or `fit-content`). For content-sized overlays, use `top/left: 50%` with `translate: -50% -50%`. A percentage inset such as `inset: 25%` also centers the box, but only when you have picked the percentage to match its size.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

previous.back:
The `inset` property is a **shorthand** for `top`, `right`, `bottom`, and `left`. This can save you from the tedious process of defining these properties individually, as you can use `inset` to set them all at once.

```html
<div class="box"></div>
```

```css
.box {
  position: absolute;
  /* top: 10px, right: 20px, bottom: 30px, left: 40px */
  inset: 10px 20px 30px 40px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`inset` is shorthand for `top`, `right`, `bottom`, and `left`. It follows the same value order as `margin`: 1 value = all sides, 2 = vertical | horizontal, 3 = top | horizontal | bottom, 4 = top | right | bottom | left (clockwise).

```css
.box {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 10px 20px 30px 40px;  /* top 10, right 20, bottom 30, left 40 */
}

.overlay {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;                     /* all four sides = 0 */
}
```

`inset` only affects positioned elements (`relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, `sticky`).

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/inset.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

(1) Cover a parent completely: a loading overlay or clickable card link with `position: absolute; inset: 0`. (2) Pin to one corner: a toast or chat bubble with `position: fixed; inset: auto 1rem 1rem auto` (bottom-right). (3) Center a fixed-size modal: `position: fixed; inset: 0; margin: auto` plus a width and height.

```css
.loading-overlay { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.7); }

.toast { position: fixed; inset: auto 1rem 1rem auto; }

.modal {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  margin: auto;
  width: min(90vw, 480px);
  height: fit-content;
}
```

All of these replace four separate `top/right/bottom/left` declarations. `inset` is physical (not affected by writing direction); for right-to-left support use `inset-inline`/`inset-block`.

