back:
Give the parent `position: relative` so it becomes the containing block, and give the child `position: absolute; inset: 0`. With all four offsets at 0, the child stretches to fill the parent's padding box.

```css
.card { position: relative; }

.card .overlay {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;               /* top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0 */
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.7);
}
```

If you forget `position: relative` on the parent, the overlay sizes itself to the nearest positioned ancestor, often the whole page. For content taller than the parent, add `overflow: hidden` on the parent or the overlay may not cover it.

