For the pulse loader, you'll need a parent container with two child elements, one for each ring. Use `@keyframes` to define an animation at two points in the cycle. At the start (`0%`), the two child elements have no `width` or `height` and are positioned at the center. At the end (`100%`), both elements have increased `width` and `height`, but their `position` is reset to `0`. Use `opacity` to transition from `1` to `0` when animating to give them a disappearing effect as they expand.

Set a predefined `width` and `height` for the parent container and use `position: relative` to position its children. Use `animation-delay` on the second child, so that each element starts its animation at a different time.

```html
<div class="ripple-loader">
  <div></div>
  <div></div>
</div>
```

```css
.ripple-loader {
  position: relative;
  width: 64px;
  height: 64px;
}

.ripple-loader div {
  position: absolute;
  border: 4px solid #454ADE;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: ripple-loader 1s ease-out infinite;
}

.ripple-loader div:nth-child(2) {
  animation-delay: -0.5s;
}

@keyframes ripple-loader {
  0% {
    top: 32px;
    left: 32px;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    opacity: 1;
  }
  100% {
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 64px;
    height: 64px;
    opacity: 0;
  }
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/loaders.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`width`/`height`/`top`/`left` change layout, so the browser recalculates layout and repaints on every frame. `transform` and `opacity` can be animated by the compositor without re-running layout, which keeps the animation smooth, especially on phones. Give the ring its full size, then scale it from 0 to 1 while fading it out. It stays centered because it scales around its own center.

```css
.ripple-loader { position: relative; width: 64px; height: 64px; }

.ripple-loader div {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  border: 4px solid #454ade;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: ripple 1s ease-out infinite;
}
.ripple-loader div:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: -0.5s; }

@keyframes ripple {
  from { transform: scale(0); opacity: 1; }
  to   { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
}
```

The negative delay on the second ring starts it halfway through its cycle, so the two rings alternate from the first frame. One difference: scaling also scales the border width, which the size-based version didn't do.

