Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/loaders.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

`padding-left` → `padding-inline-start`, `padding-right` → `padding-inline-end`, `padding-top` → `padding-block-start`, `padding-bottom` → `padding-block-end`. `padding-inline` sets left and right together, and `padding-block` sets top and bottom.

```css
.button {
  padding-block: 0.5rem;     /* top & bottom */
  padding-inline: 1rem;      /* left & right */
}

.list-item-with-icon {
  padding-inline-start: 2rem;  /* room for the icon; flips in RTL */
}
```

`padding-inline: 1rem 2rem` means start 1rem, end 2rem. For symmetric values, logical and physical give the same result in LTR. The benefit shows with asymmetric spacing (e.g. room for an icon), which then mirrors automatically in right-to-left languages.

