Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/logical-physical-properties-map.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

`margin-left` → `margin-inline-start`, `margin-right` → `margin-inline-end`, `margin-top` → `margin-block-start`, `margin-bottom` → `margin-block-end`; the shorthands `margin-inline` and `margin-block` set both ends. `margin-inline: auto` sets both inline margins to `auto`, which centers a block element that has a width, like `margin: 0 auto` but without touching the top and bottom margins.

```css
.page {
  max-inline-size: 70ch;
  margin-inline: auto;         /* center horizontally */
}

.stack > * + * {
  margin-block-start: 1rem;    /* space between stacked items */
}
```

Logical margins mirror in right-to-left languages, which matters for asymmetric spacing such as indenting from the start side.

