Tracking the mouse is relatively simple. We'll first use `Document.querySelector()` to select the button, and then use `EventTarget.addEventListener()` to register a handler for the `'mousemove'` event.

Every time the **listener is triggered**, we'll use `Element.getBoundingClientRect()` to get the button's position on the screen, and then use `CSSStyleDeclaration.setProperty()` to update the values of the `--x` and `--y` CSS variables.

When these two values change, **the gradient will update its position**, making it look like it's following the mouse cursor.

```js
const btn = document.querySelector('.mouse-cursor-gradient-tracking');

btn.addEventListener('mousemove', e => {
  const rect = e.target.getBoundingClientRect();
  const x = e.clientX - rect.left;
  const y = e.clientY - rect.top;

  btn.style.setProperty('--x', x + 'px');
  btn.style.setProperty('--y', y + 'px');
});
```

Putting everything together gives us a nice hover effect where the gradient follows the mouse cursor. You can see the final result in the CodePen below:

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/mouse-cursor-gradient-tracking.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
On `pointermove`, subtract the button's `getBoundingClientRect()` left/top from `e.clientX/clientY` to get the pointer position inside the button, and write it to `--x`/`--y` with `style.setProperty`. The CSS gradient reads those variables, e.g. `radial-gradient(circle at var(--x) var(--y), …)`. Use the button itself (or `e.currentTarget`): `e.target` is whatever child is under the pointer (here the inner `<span>`), and that child's rectangle gives wrong coordinates.

```js
const btn = document.querySelector('.gradient-button');

btn.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
  const rect = btn.getBoundingClientRect();   // not e.target
  btn.style.setProperty('--x', `${e.clientX - rect.left}px`);
  btn.style.setProperty('--y', `${e.clientY - rect.top}px`);
});
```

```css
.gradient-button::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: radial-gradient(circle at var(--x, 50%) var(--y, 50%), #ff8a00, transparent 60%);
}
```

Only the variables change, so the gradient updates without adding classes. The `var()` fallback centers the glow before the first move.

