Hiding an element with `display: none` comes with the drawback of making it inaccessible to screen readers. On the other hand, **hiding an element offscreen** allows you to keep it **accessible** while removing it from the visual and positional flow of the page. This is useful for elements that are not meant to be seen but are still required for accessibility purposes (e.g. skip links).

In order to achieve this, you will need to create your own **utility class** that will hide the element offscreen. This class will remove any `border` and `padding`, hide the element's `overflow`, and make the `height` and `width` of the element `1px` while negating them using `margin`. The element will also be positioned absolutely so that it does not take up space in the DOM.

> [!TIP]
>
> This technique provides an **accessible** and layout-friendly alternative to `display: none` (not readable by screen readers) and `visibility: hidden` (takes up physical space in the DOM).

```html
<a class="button" href="https://google.com">
  Learn More <span class="offscreen"> about pants</span>
</a>
```

```css
.offscreen {
  border: 0;
  clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
  height: 1px;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  padding: 0;
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/offscreen.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use a "visually hidden" (often called `sr-only`) utility class that shrinks the element to 1×1px, clips it, and takes it out of the layout with `position: absolute`. It stays in the accessibility tree, so screen readers still read it. `display: none` and `visibility: hidden` both remove content from the accessibility tree, and `visibility: hidden` also still takes up space.

```html
<a href="/products/pants">Learn more<span class="visually-hidden"> about pants</span></a>
```

```css
.visually-hidden {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: -1px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;    /* stop text wrapping into many 1px lines */
}
```

Older snippets use `clip: rect(0 0 0 0)`; `clip` is deprecated but still works. For skip links, undo the class on `:focus` so the link becomes visible when tabbed to.

