Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/root-vs-html.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Horizontal progress-style scroll bars are a fairly new trend in web design. They are often used in tandem with the traditional vertical scroll bar to provide a visual representation of the user's progress through the page. So how does one go about implementing this feature?

As far as CSS is concerned, there's very little work to be done. The progress bar itself is just a simple `div` element with a fixed position at the top of the page.

The magic happens in the JavaScript, where we use `EventTarget.addEventListener()` to listen for the `scroll` event. Then, we use the `Element.scrollTop` property to determine the **scroll percentage of the document** and apply it to the `width` of the progress bar.

```css
#scroll-progress {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  width: 0%;
  height: 4px;
  background: #7983ff;
  z-index: 10000;
}
```

```js
const scrollProgress = document.getElementById('scroll-progress');
const height =
  document.documentElement.scrollHeight - document.documentElement.clientHeight;

window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  const scrollTop =
    document.body.scrollTop || document.documentElement.scrollTop;
  scrollProgress.style.width = `${(scrollTop / height) * 100}%`;
});
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/scroll-progress-bar.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Fix a thin bar to the top of the viewport and, on `scroll`, set its width (or `scaleX`) to scrolled distance ÷ total scrollable distance. Total scrollable distance is `scrollHeight − clientHeight` of the document element. Measure it inside the handler, because images and lazy content change the page height after load.

```css
#scroll-progress {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0 0 auto 0;
  height: 4px;
  background: #7983ff;
  transform-origin: left;
  transform: scaleX(0);
}
```

```js
const bar = document.getElementById('scroll-progress');
const update = () => {
  const el = document.documentElement;
  const max = el.scrollHeight - el.clientHeight;
  bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? window.scrollY / max : 0})`;
};
window.addEventListener('scroll', update, { passive: true });
update();
```

`scaleX` avoids re-running layout, unlike changing `width`. In supporting browsers, CSS scroll-driven animations (`animation-timeline: scroll()`) can do this with no JavaScript. The bar is decorative, so give it `aria-hidden="true"`.

