To prevent the default browser **behavior when reaching the end of the scrollable container**, you can use the `overscroll-behavior` property. This property can be set to `auto`, `contain`, or `none`.

The `contain` value is usually preferred as it allows the scroll snap to work as expected when scrolling over the edge of the container.

> [!TIP]
>
> Depending on your use case, you can use `overscroll-behavior-x` and `overscroll-behavior-y` to control the behavior on the horizontal and vertical axes respectively, or even `overscroll-behavior-inline` and `overscroll-behavior-block` if you're using Logical Properties.

```css
/* Horizontal scroll snap */
.scroll-snap {
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
}

/* Vertical scroll snap */
.scroll-snap {
  scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
  overscroll-behavior-y: contain;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/scroll-snap.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
It stops scroll chaining. When the carousel hits its end, extra scroll momentum is not passed to the page or parent scroller, and browser overscroll gestures such as swipe-to-go-back (horizontal) or pull-to-refresh (vertical) are not triggered. It does not affect the snapping itself.

```css
.carousel {
  overflow-x: auto;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;   /* don't hand extra scroll to the page */
}
```

Values: `auto` (default: chain to the parent), `contain` (no chaining; the element's own bounce effect remains), `none` (no chaining and no bounce). Per-axis versions exist (`-x`/`-y`, or logical `-inline`/`-block`). The same property keeps a scrollable modal or side panel from scrolling the page behind it.

