Putting everything together, we can create a horizontally scrollable container that will snap on elements when scrolling. At its core the HTML and CSS is quite simple. All you need to do is set the `scroll-snap-type` to `x mandatory` and the `overscroll-behavior-x` to `contain`. You can decide on the value of your `scroll-snap-align` based on your design.

```html
<div class="horizontal-snap">
  <!-- Add your content here, use .element for snap points -->
</div>
```

```css
.horizontal-snap {
  overflow-x: auto;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
}

.horizontal-snap > .element {
  scroll-snap-align: center; /* or start, end */
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/scroll-snap.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Three things on the container (`overflow-x: auto`, `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory`, and optionally `overscroll-behavior-x: contain`) plus `scroll-snap-align` on each card. A flex row with `flex: none` keeps the cards from shrinking to fit.

```css
.horizontal-snap {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
  overflow-x: auto;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;   /* don't trigger back-swipe at the ends */
}

.horizontal-snap > .card {
  flex: none;
  inline-size: 80%;
  scroll-snap-align: center;        /* or start / end */
}
```

Without `flex: none` (or a fixed width), flex items shrink to fit the container, nothing overflows, and there is nothing to scroll or snap. Keyboard and screen-reader users should still be able to reach every card, e.g. with prev/next buttons.

