Styling links with CSS is considered straightforward, with most developers using the `:link` and `:visited` pseudo-classes. While this solution is very common, there's a less verbose alternative in the form of the `:any-link` pseudo-class. This pseudo-class selects all links, regardless of whether they have been visited or not. Thus, it acts as a catch-all for all links on the page.

```css
:any-link {
  color: #0444f6;
}
```

One important note is that using `:any-link` is different to using the `[href]` attribute selector. The `:any-link` pseudo-class does not select empty links, whereas the `[href]` attribute selector does.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/select-any-link.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`:any-link` matches every `<a>` or `<area>` element that has an `href`, whether visited or not. It is the same as `:link, :visited` in one selector. `[href]` matches any element with an `href` attribute, including `<link>` and `<base>` in the head, so a bare `[href]` rule is broader than you might expect.

```css
:any-link {
  color: #0444f6;
}

/* Equivalent to */
:link, :visited { color: #0444f6; }
```

Neither matches an `<a>` without `href` (a placeholder anchor), which is not a link. `<a href="">` is a link to the current page, so both selectors match it.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/select-any-link.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

