A simple CSS square can be created by setting the `width` and `height` to the same value. Similarly, a rectangle can be created by setting the `width` and `height` to different values.

```html
<div class="square"></div>
<div class="rectangle"></div>
```

```css
.square {
  width: 64px;
  height: 64px;
  background: #5394fd;
}

.rectangle {
  width: 128px;
  height: 64px;
  background: #5394fd;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/shapes.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Set a fluid `width` and `aspect-ratio: 1`; the browser derives the height from the width. Fixed squares and rectangles just need equal or unequal `width` and `height`, but `aspect-ratio` keeps the shape as the width changes (e.g. `16 / 9` for video thumbnails).

```css
.square {
  width: 25%;
  aspect-ratio: 1;        /* height follows width */
  background: #5394fd;
}

.video-thumb {
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
}
```

`aspect-ratio` replaced the old `padding-top: 56.25%` hack, which worked because percentage padding is based on the width. If content is taller than the ratio allows, the box grows unless you also set `overflow: hidden` or a `min-height: 0`.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/shapes.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

