Using the `border-radius` property, a circle can be created by setting it to `50%`. The `width` and `height` must be the same to create a circle. Subsequently, an ellipse can be created by providing two values for the `border-radius` and setting the `width` and `height` to different values.

```html
<div class="circle"></div>
<div class="ellipse"></div>
```

```css
.circle {
  width: 64px;
  height: 64px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: #5394fd;
}

.ellipse {
  width: 128px;
  height: 64px;
  border-radius: 64px / 32px;
  background: #5394fd;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/shapes.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use `border-radius: 50%` in both cases. On a square it produces a circle; on a non-square rectangle the same `50%` produces an ellipse, because the percentage is calculated separately against the width (horizontal radius) and the height (vertical radius).

```css
.circle {
  width: 64px;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: 50%;
}

.ellipse {
  width: 128px;
  height: 64px;
  border-radius: 50%;   /* same as 64px / 32px */
}
```

The slash syntax (`border-radius: 64px / 32px`) sets horizontal / vertical radii explicitly when you need something else. A large fixed value like `border-radius: 9999px` gives a "pill" (rounded ends, straight sides), not an ellipse.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/shapes.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

