Both lower the opacity of all items that are not hovered. `.container:hover` matches as soon as the pointer is anywhere over the container, including the gaps between items, so every item fades even when none is hovered. `.container:has(.item:hover)` only matches while an actual item is hovered, so the fade appears only when it should.

```css
.item { transition: opacity 0.3s; }

/* Fades everything when the pointer is in a gap */
.container:hover .item:not(:hover) { opacity: 0.5; }

/* Fades siblings only while an item is hovered */
.container:has(.item:hover) .item:not(:hover) { opacity: 0.5; }
```

`:has()` works in all modern browsers. Consider adding `:focus-visible` (`.container:has(.item:focus-visible)`) so keyboard users get the same highlight, and keep the faded items readable (contrast).

