It makes the page use each operating system's native UI font (San Francisco on Apple devices, Segoe UI on Windows, Roboto on Android, Ubuntu/Cantarell on Linux) with no web-font download, by listing each OS's font name in order. `system-ui` now does this in every modern browser, so the long list is mainly for very old browsers.

```css
/* Legacy stack */
body {
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto,
    Oxygen-Sans, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;
}

/* Today */
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
```

`-apple-system` and `BlinkMacSystemFont` are non-standard aliases for San Francisco in Safari and older Chrome. Always end a stack with a generic family (`sans-serif`) as the last fallback.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/system-font-stack.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

`system-ui` is a generic font family that resolves to the operating system's default UI font (Segoe UI, San Francisco, Roboto…). Your app looks native, loads no web font, and avoids a flash of unstyled text. `sans-serif` is the fallback. It is part of CSS Fonts Level 4 and supported in all modern browsers.

```css
body {
  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
}
```

Trade-off: the font differs per device, so line lengths and text wrapping vary between platforms, and you give up a consistent brand typeface. Test layouts on at least Windows and iOS/Android.

