As specified in the custom property notation, CSS variables are **prefixed with two dashes** (e.g. `--my-color`). To access them, you can use the `var()` function (e.g. `var(--my-color)`). CSS variables are exceptionally useful for **sharing styles** between different elements and components. Examples include but are not limited to vertical rhythm, typography variables and color palettes.

```css
/* Define a variable */
:root {
  --my-color: #f0c1a8;
}

/* Use the variable */
body {
  color: var(--my-color);
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/variables.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Declare it with a name starting with two dashes (`--brand: #0a66c2;`), usually on `:root` for global values. Read it with `var(--brand)`. A second argument is the fallback used when the property isn't set: `var(--brand, blue)`.

```css
:root {
  --brand: #0a66c2;
  --space-3: 1rem;
}

.button {
  background: var(--brand);
  padding: var(--space-3);
  border-radius: var(--radius, 6px);   /* fallback if --radius is unset */
}
```

Custom properties inherit and cascade like normal properties, so a component can override them locally (`.danger { --brand: #b00020; }`). Names are case-sensitive (`--Brand` ≠ `--brand`). JavaScript can change them with `el.style.setProperty('--brand', 'red')`.

