Zebra striped **lists** and **tables** are a great way to improve the **readability** of your content. By using the `:nth-child(odd)` or `:nth-child(even)` pseudo-class selectors, you can apply different background colors to elements based on their position in a group of siblings.

> [!TIP]
>
> You can use this trick to apply different styles to **any other elements** that are part of a group of siblings.

```css
/* Zebra striped list */
li:nth-child(odd) {
  background-color: #ccc;
}

/* Zebra striped table */
tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: #ccc;
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/css/s/zebra-striped-list-or-table.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use `tbody tr:nth-child(even)` (or `odd`) to give alternate rows a background. `:nth-child` counts all siblings, including hidden ones, so after filtering out rows (e.g. with the `hidden` attribute), two visible neighbors can get the same color. Fix it with the `of S` syntax, which counts only the rows that match: `:nth-child(even of :not([hidden]))`.

```css
tbody tr:nth-child(even) {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
}

/* Stripes stay correct when rows are hidden */
tbody tr:nth-child(even of :not([hidden])) {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
}
```

Keep the stripe color subtle and check text contrast on both row colors. The same pattern works for list items or any group of siblings.

