back:
Define keyframes that start and end at normal size and scale up in the middle (`0%`/`100%` → `scale: 1`, `50%` → `scale: 1.5`), then apply them with `animation: … infinite`. Because the first and last frames match, each loop starts where the previous one ended, with no jump. Add a `prefers-reduced-motion` rule that stops it.

```css
.zoom-in-out {
  animation: zoom-in-zoom-out 1s ease infinite;
}

@keyframes zoom-in-zoom-out {
  0%, 100% { scale: 1; }
  50%      { scale: 1.5; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .zoom-in-out { animation: none; }
}
```

The standalone `scale` property works in all modern browsers; `transform: scale(1.5)` is equivalent. Endless motion distracts users and can cause discomfort for people with vestibular disorders. Keep it for brief attention cues, not permanent decoration.

