On a side note, you can also **prerender pages**. Prerendering is a more aggressive form of prefetching that not only fetches the resources for a page but also renders it in the background. This means that when the user eventually navigates to the prerendered page, it can be displayed almost instantly.

The only difference code-wise is that you specify `"prerender"` instead of `"prefetch"` in your speculation rules.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{
  "prerender": [
    {
      "urls": ["/home", "/about", "/contact"]
    }
  ]
}
</script>
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/html/s/speculation-rules-api-first-look.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use a `"prerender"` rule instead of `"prefetch"`. The browser fetches the page *and* renders it, running its JavaScript, in a hidden tab, so navigation feels instant.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{ "prerender": [{ "urls": ["/dashboard", "/orders"] }] }
</script>
```

Because scripts run before the user ever sees the page, page-view analytics or "mark as read" calls could fire for pages never visited. Check `document.prerendering` and defer such work until the `prerenderingchange` event.

```js
if (document.prerendering) {
  document.addEventListener('prerenderingchange', trackPageView, { once: true });
} else {
  trackPageView();
}
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/html/s/speculation-rules-api-first-look.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

Use `"where"` with an `"and"` of conditions: `href_matches` (URL Pattern syntax, `*` wildcards) to include links, and `"not"` clauses to exclude URL patterns or CSS selectors.

```html
<script type="speculationrules">
{
  "prefetch": [{
    "where": { "and": [
      { "href_matches": "/*" },
      { "not": { "href_matches": "/logout" } },
      { "not": { "href_matches": "/*\\?*(^|&)add-to-cart=*" } },
      { "not": { "selector_matches": "[rel~=nofollow]" } }
    ] },
    "eagerness": "moderate"
  }]
}
</script>
```

Exclusions matter because a prefetch is a real GET request: a `/logout` or `?add-to-cart=` link with side effects would run just because the user hovered it. The deeper fix is to never change state on GET. Use a list of `"urls"` instead when you know the exact pages.

---
