Starting off with a simple one, the `useToggler` hook provides a **boolean state variable** that can be toggled between its two states. Instead of managing the state manually, you can simply call the `toggleValue` function to toggle the state.

The implementation is rather simple, as well. You use the `useState()` hook to create the `value` state variable and its setter. Then, you create a function that toggles the value of the state variable and memoize it, using the `useCallback()` hook. Finally, you return the `value` state variable and the memoized function.

```jsx
const useToggler = initialState => {
  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(initialState);
  const toggleValue = React.useCallback(() => setValue(prev => !prev), []);

  return [value, toggleValue];
};

const Switch = () => {
  const [val, toggleVal] = useToggler(false);
  return <button onClick={toggleVal}>{val ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}</button>;
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <Switch />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/advanced-react-state-hooks.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Keep the boolean in `useState`, and wrap a **functional update** (`setValue(prev => !prev)`) in `useCallback` with an empty dependency array. Because the updater reads the previous value from React, the function needs no dependencies, so it is created once and stays stable.

```jsx
function useToggler(initial = false) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initial);
  const toggle = useCallback(() => setValue(prev => !prev), []);
  return [value, toggle];
}

function DarkModeSwitch() {
  const [on, toggle] = useToggler(false);
  return <button onClick={toggle}>{on ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}</button>;
}
```

Pitfall: writing `() => setValue(!value)` would capture `value` from one render. You would then have to list `value` as a dependency (so the function changes every toggle), or it goes stale. A stable `toggle` only matters when you pass it to memoized children or effects.

