React decides whether state changed with `Object.is(old, new)`. Mutating the existing `Map` keeps the same object, so React sees "no change" and skips the render. Instead, create a **new** `Map` from the previous one, change the copy, and return it from a functional update.

```jsx
function useMap(entries) {
  const [map, setMap] = useState(() => new Map(entries)); // lazy init: built once
  const actions = useMemo(() => ({
    set: (key, value) => setMap(prev => new Map(prev).set(key, value)),
    remove: key => setMap(prev => {
      const next = new Map(prev);
      next.delete(key);
      return next;
    }),
    clear: () => setMap(new Map()),
  }), []);
  return [map, actions];
}

// const [cart, { set, remove }] = useMap([['apples', 10]]);
```

Notes: `Map.prototype.set` returns the map, so `new Map(prev).set(k, v)` is a one-liner. `useState(() => new Map(entries))` avoids building a throwaway `Map` on every render. `actions` has no dependencies because the state setter is stable. The same copy-then-change rule applies to `Set`, arrays, and objects.

