Map the column names twice: once for the header cells, and once inside each row to read `row[col]`. There's no need to filter or copy the objects first; you just don't read the columns you don't show.

```jsx
function MappedTable({ rows, columns }) {
  return (
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>{columns.map(col => <th key={col}>{col}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(row => (
          <tr key={row.id}>
            {columns.map(col => <td key={col}>{row[col]}</td>)}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  );
}
```

Gotchas: a missing property is `undefined` and renders as an empty cell. A nested object value throws "Objects are not valid as a React child", so format it first (e.g. `String(v)` or a per-column render function). Use a real row ID as the key when one exists.

