A variation of the `useEventListener` hook is the `useHover` hook, which listens for the `'mouseover'` and `'mouseout'` events on a given element. As these mouse events are common, this hook can be useful for handling hover states.

The implementation is similar to the `useEventListener` hook, but it uses two separate handlers for `'mouseover'` and `'mouseout'` events. It also uses a ref to keep track of the last node passed to the `callbackRef` function, allowing it to remove the listeners when the component unmounts.

```jsx
const useHover = () => {
  const [isHovering, setIsHovering] = React.useState(false);

  const handleMouseOver = React.useCallback(() => setIsHovering(true), []);
  const handleMouseOut = React.useCallback(() => setIsHovering(false), []);

  const nodeRef = React.useRef();

  const callbackRef = React.useCallback(
    node => {
      if (nodeRef.current) {
        nodeRef.current.removeEventListener('mouseover', handleMouseOver);
        nodeRef.current.removeEventListener('mouseout', handleMouseOut);
      }

      nodeRef.current = node;

      if (nodeRef.current) {
        nodeRef.current.addEventListener('mouseover', handleMouseOver);
        nodeRef.current.addEventListener('mouseout', handleMouseOut);
      }
    },
    [handleMouseOver, handleMouseOut]
  );

  return [callbackRef, isHovering];
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const [hoverRef, isHovering] = useHover();

  return <div ref={hoverRef}>{isHovering ? 'Hovering' : 'Not hovering'}</div>;
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/event-listener-hooks.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Return a **callback ref**: a function React calls with the DOM node when it attaches. In React 19 that function can return a cleanup, which React calls when the node is detached, so you no longer need an extra ref to remember and unsubscribe the previous node.

```jsx
function useHover() {
  const [hovering, setHovering] = useState(false);
  const ref = useCallback(node => {
    const enter = () => setHovering(true);
    const leave = () => setHovering(false);
    node.addEventListener('mouseenter', enter);
    node.addEventListener('mouseleave', leave);
    return () => {
      node.removeEventListener('mouseenter', enter);
      node.removeEventListener('mouseleave', leave);
    };
  }, []);
  return [ref, hovering];
}

// const [ref, hovering] = useHover();  <div ref={ref}>...</div>
```

Use `mouseenter`/`mouseleave`: `mouseover`/`mouseout` bubble and fire when moving between child elements, which makes the state flicker. `useCallback` keeps the ref stable; a new ref function each render would detach and re-attach. For a single element, `onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave` props (or CSS `:hover` for styling only) are simpler.

