`[]` means "this effect depends on no values", so it runs after the first commit to the screen and never re-runs; its cleanup (if any) runs on unmount. In development, **StrictMode** deliberately mounts, cleans up, and mounts again, so the setup runs twice to expose effects that lack proper cleanup. Production runs it once.

```jsx
function useComponentDidMount(onMount) {
  useEffect(() => {
    onMount();
  }, []); // run once after mount (twice in dev StrictMode)
}

function Page() {
  useComponentDidMount(() => console.log('mounted'));
  return <div>Check the console!</div>;
}
```

Effects are not lifecycle methods: think "synchronize with something outside React", not "on mount". If running twice breaks your code (double subscription, duplicate POST), the fix is a cleanup function, or moving the action to the event handler that caused it, not suppressing StrictMode.

