In development, StrictMode runs setup → cleanup → setup on mount, and refs survive that cycle. The first setup sets `mounted.current = true`, so the second setup already thinks it's an update and fires the callback on mount. Usually, do the work in the **event handler** that changed the value. If you truly need "run when X changes after mount", compare against the previous value instead of counting runs.

```jsx
function useOnUpdate(value, callback) {
  const prev = useRef(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (Object.is(prev.current, value)) return; // mount, or re-run with same value
    prev.current = value;
    callback(value);
  }, [value]);
}

// Often better: no effect at all
const increment = () => {
  const next = value + 1;
  setValue(next);
  console.log(`Value is now ${next}`);
};
```

Why prefer the handler: the effect version runs one render later and hides the cause of the change. Keep effects for syncing with outside systems.

