Put the address after `mailto:` and add `subject` and `body` as query parameters, each encoded with `encodeURIComponent`. It encodes spaces as `%20`, which every mail client understands; `URLSearchParams` encodes spaces as `+`, which some mail apps show literally ("Hello+%26+Welcome").

```jsx
function Mailto({ email, subject = '', body = '', children }) {
  const params = [];
  if (subject) params.push(`subject=${encodeURIComponent(subject)}`);
  if (body) params.push(`body=${encodeURIComponent(body)}`);
  const query = params.length ? `?${params.join('&')}` : '';
  return <a href={`mailto:${email}${query}`}>{children}</a>;
}

// <Mailto email="support@example.com" subject="Hello & Welcome">Email us</Mailto>
// href: mailto:support@example.com?subject=Hello%20%26%20Welcome
```

Without encoding, the `&` in "Hello & Welcome" would start a new parameter and cut the subject short. A `mailto:` link only opens the user's mail app; it doesn't send anything.

