In order to persist a stateful value to `localStorage`, you can create a custom hook called `useLocalStorage`. This hook will return a stateful value and a function to update it.

Implementing the hook requires you to use the `useState()` hook with a function to initialize its value lazily. You can then use a `try...catch` block and `Storage.getItem()` to try and get the value from `Window.localStorage`. If no value is found, use `Storage.setItem()` to store the `defaultValue` and use it as the initial state. If an error occurs, use `defaultValue` as the initial state. Finally, define a function that will update the state variable with the passed value and use `Storage.setItem()` to store it.

```jsx
const useLocalStorage = (keyName, defaultValue) => {
  const [storedValue, setStoredValue] = React.useState(() => {
    try {
      const value = window.localStorage.getItem(keyName);

      if (value) {
        return JSON.parse(value);
      } else {
        window.localStorage.setItem(keyName, JSON.stringify(defaultValue));
        return defaultValue;
      }
    } catch (err) {
      return defaultValue;
    }
  });

  const setValue = newValue => {
    try {
      window.localStorage.setItem(keyName, JSON.stringify(newValue));
    } catch (err) {}
    setStoredValue(newValue);
  };

  return [storedValue, setValue];
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const [name, setName] = useLocalStorage('name', 'John');

  return <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/persist-state-to-storage.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Pass a **function** to `useState` (lazy initialization) so `localStorage.getItem` and `JSON.parse` run only on the first render. Wrap storage access in `try/catch` because it can throw: storage may be disabled or full (`QuotaExceededError`), and the stored text may not be valid JSON.

```jsx
function useLocalStorage(key, defaultValue) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    try {
      const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
      return raw !== null ? JSON.parse(raw) : defaultValue;
    } catch {
      return defaultValue;
    }
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
    } catch {}
  }, [key, value]);

  return [value, setValue];
}

// const [theme, setTheme] = useLocalStorage('theme', 'light');
```

Writing in an effect means `setValue(prev => ...)` works like normal state. Limits: changing `key` doesn't reload the value, other tabs aren't synced (listen for the `storage` event), and `localStorage` doesn't exist during server rendering.

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/persist-state-to-storage.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

