The exact same logic can be applied to persist a stateful value to `sessionStorage`. You can create a custom hook called `useSessionStorage` that returns a stateful value and a function to update it. The only difference is that you'll be using `Window.sessionStorage` instead of `Window.localStorage`.

```jsx
const useSessionStorage = (keyName, defaultValue) => {
  const [storedValue, setStoredValue] = React.useState(() => {
    try {
      const value = window.sessionStorage.getItem(keyName);

      if (value) {
        return JSON.parse(value);
      } else {
        window.sessionStorage.setItem(keyName, JSON.stringify(defaultValue));
        return defaultValue;
      }
    } catch (err) {
      return defaultValue;
    }
  });

  const setValue = newValue => {
    try {
      window.sessionStorage.setItem(keyName, JSON.stringify(newValue));
    } catch (err) {}
    setStoredValue(newValue);
  };

  return [storedValue, setValue];
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const [name, setName] = useSessionStorage('name', 'John');

  return <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/persist-state-to-storage.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`sessionStorage` is scoped to **one tab** and is cleared when that tab closes; `localStorage` is shared by all tabs of the same origin and survives browser restarts. Choose `sessionStorage` for data that belongs to one visit, such as a half-filled multi-step form, so it survives a reload but doesn't leak into other tabs or next week.

```jsx
function useStoredState(key, defaultValue, storage = sessionStorage) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    try {
      const raw = storage.getItem(key);
      return raw !== null ? JSON.parse(raw) : defaultValue;
    } catch {
      return defaultValue;
    }
  });
  useEffect(() => {
    try { storage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value)); } catch {}
  }, [key, value, storage]);
  return [value, setValue];
}

// const [step, setStep] = useStoredState('checkout-step', 1);
```

Both APIs store strings only (hence `JSON.stringify`), are synchronous, and are readable by any script on the page, so never store tokens or secrets there.

