The `PropTypes.objectOf()` validator is used when describing an object whose **keys might not be known ahead of time**, and often represent the same type. For example:

```js
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

// Expected prop object - dynamic keys (i.e. user ids)
const myProp = {
  25891102: 'johnsmith',
  34712915: 'ducklord',
  76912999: 'mrquacks'
};

// PropTypes validation for the prop object
MyComponent.propTypes = {
  myProp: PropTypes.objectOf(PropTypes.number)
};
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/proptypes-objectof-vs-shape.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Use `Record<string, string>` (or an index signature `{ [userId: string]: string }`): any string key, every value a string. React 19 removed `propTypes` checking entirely, so `Component.propTypes` is silently ignored; use TypeScript for compile-time checks instead.

```tsx
type Props = {
  usernamesById: Record<string, string>;
};

function UserList({ usernamesById }: Props) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {Object.entries(usernamesById).map(([id, name]) => (
        <li key={id}>{name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Old PropTypes form: PropTypes.objectOf(PropTypes.string)
```

Note: types vanish at runtime. For data from an API, validate it where it enters the app (e.g. with a schema library such as Zod) rather than trusting the type.

