A misspelled string like `'setNmae'` silently matches no `case`, so the reducer returns the old state and nothing happens. Constants give one definition, editor autocomplete, and one place to rename. `createSlice` goes further: it generates the action types and action creators from your reducer names, so you never write the strings yourself.

```js
const ACTION_TYPES = {
  SET_NAME: 'user/setName',
  ADD_TAG: 'user/addTag',
};

dispatch({ type: ACTION_TYPES.SET_NAME, payload: 'Ada' });

// Redux Toolkit
const userSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'user',
  initialState: { name: '', tags: [] },
  reducers: { setName: (state, action) => { state.name = action.payload; } },
});
dispatch(userSlice.actions.setName('Ada')); // type: 'user/setName'
```

With `useReducer` in TypeScript, a union type of actions (`{ type: 'setName'; payload: string } | ...`) gives the same typo protection.

