Give every action the same shape, `{ type, payload }`; move each case's logic into a small named handler (or let Redux Toolkit's `createSlice` generate the reducer); and define action types once as constants or generated names. The reducer then reads like a table of contents.

```js
const handlers = {
  'user/setName': (state, name) => ({ ...state, name }),
  'user/addTag': (state, tag) => ({ ...state, tags: [...state.tags, tag] }),
};

function userReducer(state, { type, payload }) {
  const handle = handlers[type];
  return handle ? handle(state, payload) : state;
}

dispatch({ type: 'user/addTag', payload: 'react' });
```

Why the shape matters: with a fixed `payload` key, nobody has to remember whether this action used `action.name`, `action.value`, or `action.data`. Small handlers are easy to test on their own. In Redux apps, `createSlice` is the standard way to get all three.

