By default **all of them, recursively**, whether or not their props changed. Rendering just means calling the components again; React then compares the results with the previous render and touches the DOM only where the output actually changed.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Sidebar />        {/* re-renders on every click, though it has no props */}
    </>
  );
}
```

Ways to skip work: wrap a child in `React.memo` (skip when props are equal), or let the React Compiler memoize automatically. Elements passed in as `children` from higher up are not re-created when this component's state changes, so they don't re-render. Usually extra renders are cheap; optimize only when profiling shows a slow component.

