Each `useSelector` subscribes to the store by itself. After every dispatch it runs its selector and re-renders its component **only if the selected result changed** (compared with `===`). Context, by contrast, re-renders every consumer whenever the provider's `value` changes. The cost: every mounted selector runs on every dispatch, so selectors must be cheap and must not return new objects each time.

```jsx
// Re-renders on EVERY dispatch: filter() returns a new array each time
const done = useSelector(state => state.todos.filter(t => t.done));

// Better: select plain values, or memoize the derived array
const doneCount = useSelector(state => state.todos.filter(t => t.done).length);
const selectDone = createSelector([s => s.todos], todos => todos.filter(t => t.done));
const done2 = useSelector(selectDone);
```

`mapStateToProps` with `connect` works the same way, but hooks are the recommended API for new code.

