React compares the provider's `value` with `Object.is`. The object literal is a **new object on every render**, so React treats the context as changed and re-renders every component that reads it, even if `user` is the same. Memoize the value so it only changes when its contents do.

```jsx
const UserContext = createContext(null);

function UserProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return <UserContext value={value}>{children}</UserContext>;
}

function Avatar() {
  const { user } = use(UserContext);   // or useContext(UserContext)
  return <img src={user?.avatarUrl} alt="" />;
}
```

React 19 lets you render `<UserContext value={...}>` directly (`.Provider` still works) and read it with `use()`. Context itself only passes a value down; the state still lives in `useState`/`useReducer`.

