They only help when something **compares the reference**: a child wrapped in `React.memo`, or a dependency array of another hook (`useEffect`, `useMemo`). Passed to a normal child, a memoized callback changes nothing, because that child re-renders with its parent anyway.

```jsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ item, onDelete }) { /* ... */ });

function List({ items }) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');
  // Stable reference, so memo(Row) can skip rows when only `filter` changes
  const handleDelete = useCallback(id => deleteItem(id), []);
  return items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} onDelete={handleDelete} />);
}
```

Also use `useMemo` for genuinely expensive calculations (e.g. sorting thousands of rows). Elsewhere they add code and memory for no gain. If your project uses the React Compiler, it inserts this memoization automatically, so manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` is rarely needed.

