Because a React component should be a **pure function of its props, state, and context**: the same inputs always produce the same output, so re-running it can't produce anything new. `React.memo`, `useMemo`, and the React Compiler all rely on that. Your render code must not read changing outside values or mutate anything.

```jsx
// Impure: output depends on something React doesn't track
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <p>Hi {name}, it is {new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}</p>;
}

// Pure: the time comes in as state/props, so React knows when it changes
function Greeting({ name, now }) {
  return <p>Hi {name}, it is {now.toLocaleTimeString()}</p>;
}
```

If a memoized component is impure, skipping it shows stale UI. StrictMode calls components twice in development to help you catch impure rendering.

