Rendering is React **calling your components** to get a description of the UI: a tree of plain JavaScript objects (React elements) produced from JSX. React compares that new tree with the tree from the **previous render**, not with the live DOM. This comparison is called reconciliation, and only the differences are then applied to the DOM.

```jsx
function Price({ amount }) {
  return <span className="price">${amount}</span>;
}
// Rendering <Price amount={5} /> produces roughly:
// { type: 'span', props: { className: 'price', children: ['$', 5] } }
```

So "a component rendered" does not mean "the page changed". If the new output equals the old one, React makes no DOM updates at all. JSX compiles to `jsx()` calls from `react/jsx-runtime` (older setups used `React.createElement`).

