Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/rendering.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

A `connect`ed component skips rendering when its parent re-renders with the same props. A component using `useSelector` re-renders **whenever its parent re-renders** (and also when its selected store data changes). If that matters, wrap the hook-based component in `React.memo`.

```jsx
const TodoItem = memo(function TodoItem({ id }) {
  const todo = useSelector(state => state.todos.byId[id]);
  return <li>{todo.title}</li>;
});
```

Hooks (`useSelector`, `useDispatch`) are the recommended API for new code; `connect` is the older higher-order-component API that you'll still meet in existing apps. Either way, passing new object or function references as props defeats the memoization.

