Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/rendering.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

**Render phase:** React calls your components and works out what changed. It doesn't touch the DOM, and it may pause, restart, or (in StrictMode) repeat this work, so rendering must be pure. **Commit phase:** React applies the changes to the DOM and attaches refs. `useLayoutEffect` runs right after the commit, **before** the browser paints; `useEffect` usually runs **after** the paint.

```jsx
function Tooltip() {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    // DOM exists, screen not painted yet: safe to measure and reposition
    console.log(ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().height);
  }, []);
  useEffect(() => {
    // after paint: subscriptions, logging, network sync
  }, []);
  return <div ref={ref}>Hi</div>;
}
```

Takeaways: rendering is not the same as updating the DOM, and a component can render without any visible change. Prefer `useEffect`; use `useLayoutEffect` only for measuring or adjusting layout before the user sees it.

