back:
Do everything in the click handler: measure the button, store the ripple's position with a unique id, and remove it in the ripple's `onAnimationEnd`. Use `e.currentTarget` (the element the handler is on, the button) because `e.target` can be the inner label `<span>`, which would give the wrong rectangle.

```jsx
function RippleButton({ children, onClick }) {
  const [ripple, setRipple] = useState(null);

  const handleClick = e => {
    const rect = e.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
    setRipple({ x: e.clientX - rect.left, y: e.clientY - rect.top, id: Date.now() });
    onClick?.(e);
  };

  return (
    <button type="button" className="ripple-button" onClick={handleClick}>
      {ripple && (
        <span
          key={ripple.id}                 // new key → remount → animation restarts
          className="ripple"
          aria-hidden="true"
          style={{ left: ripple.x, top: ripple.y }}
          onAnimationEnd={() => setRipple(null)}
        />
      )}
      <span className="content">{children}</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

Effects that only react to your own state changes ("when coords change, set isRippling") add renders and bugs; here the old `setTimeout` was never cleared and could fire after unmount.

