Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/selected-option.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Map the pairs to `<option>` elements and set the selection on the `<select>` itself: `value` (controlled, parent owns the choice) or `defaultValue` (uncontrolled, the browser owns it after the first render). Use `defaultValue` when the parent only needs to hear about changes or read the value on submit.

```jsx
function Select({ options, value, defaultValue, onValueChange, ...rest }) {
  return (
    <select
      value={value}
      defaultValue={defaultValue}
      onChange={e => onValueChange?.(e.target.value)}
      {...rest}
    >
      {options.map(([val, label]) => (
        <option key={val} value={val}>{label}</option>
      ))}
    </select>
  );
}

// Controlled:   <Select options={fruits} value={fruit} onValueChange={setFruit} />
// Uncontrolled: <Select options={fruits} defaultValue="lime" name="fruit" />
```

Pass only one of the two. `value` without `onChange` makes the select read-only, and changing `defaultValue` after mount does nothing. `e.target.value` is always a string, even if your option values started as numbers.

