React manages a select's choice through the **`value` (or `defaultValue`) prop on `<select>`**, like any other form field. `selected` on an option is the plain-HTML way, and React warns "Use the `defaultValue` or `value` props on `<select>` instead of setting `selected` on `<option>`".

```jsx
function StatusSelect({ status, onChange }) {
  return (
    <select value={status} onChange={e => onChange(e.target.value)}>
      <option value="draft">Draft</option>
      <option value="published">Published</option>
      <option value="archived">Archived</option>
    </select>
  );
}

// Multiple choice: <select multiple value={['draft', 'archived']} onChange={...}>
```

Keeping the choice in one prop means you don't have to compute `selected` for every option, and the controlled value always matches state. For `multiple`, pass an array and read `Array.from(e.target.selectedOptions, o => o.value)`.

