Because React events **bubble**: an `onClick` on the container already fires for clicks on any of its children. This is event delegation, and it needs no ref, no document listener, and no cleanup. Use `e.target.closest(...)` to find which child was clicked.

```jsx
function TagList({ tags, onRemove }) {
  const handleClick = e => {
    const button = e.target.closest('button[data-tag]');
    if (button) onRemove(button.dataset.tag);
  };
  return (
    <ul onClick={handleClick}>
      {tags.map(t => (
        <li key={t}>
          {t} <button type="button" data-tag={t} aria-label={`Remove ${t}`}>×</button>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

A document-level listener only makes sense for clicks React can't see (non-React DOM, or the "click outside" case). In most lists, a small inline handler per item (`onClick={() => onRemove(t)}`) is also perfectly fine.

