A ref holds a "has run" flag that survives re-renders without causing one. The effect re-runs whenever `when` changes, but only calls `callback` if `when` is true and the flag is still false; then it sets the flag. Refs also survive StrictMode's extra development setup, so the callback still runs once.

```jsx
function useEffectOnce(callback, when) {
  const hasRun = useRef(false);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (when && !hasRun.current) {
      hasRun.current = true;
      callback();
    }
  }, [when]);
}

// Good use: react to data arriving from outside
useEffectOnce(() => analytics.track('results_shown'), results.length > 0);
```

If `when` becomes true because the user did something (a click, a submit), run the code directly in that event handler: it's simpler and runs at the right moment. Also note that the effect uses whichever `callback` was passed on the render when `when` flipped.

