For the **search params**, the process is similar but a little more involved. You can create a custom hook that uses the `useState()` hook to lazily get the value of a specific search param using the `URLSearchParams()` constructor.

Then, use the `useCallback()` hook to create a handler that updates the state. Finally, use the `useEffect()` hook to add listeners for the `'popstate'`, `'pushstate'`, and `'replacestate'` events when mounting and clean them up when unmounting.

```jsx
const useSearchParam = param => {
  const getValue = React.useCallback(
    () => new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get(param),
    [param]
  );

  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(getValue);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    const onChange = () => {
      setValue(getValue());
    };

    window.addEventListener('popstate', onChange);
    window.addEventListener('pushstate', onChange);
    window.addEventListener('replacestate', onChange);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('popstate', onChange);
      window.removeEventListener('pushstate', onChange);
      window.removeEventListener('replacestate', onChange);
    };
  }, []);

  return value;
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const post = useSearchParam('post');

  return (
    <>
      <p>Post param value: {post || 'null'}</p>
      <button
        onClick={() =>
          history.pushState({}, '', location.pathname + '?post=42')
        }
      >
        View post 42
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => history.pushState({}, '', location.pathname)}>
        Exit
      </button>
    </>
  );
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/use-hash-search-param.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
There are no `pushstate` or `replacestate` events. `history.pushState()` changes the URL silently, and `popstate` fires only on back/forward navigation. Route your own URL changes through one function that also notifies subscribers, and read the param with `useSyncExternalStore`.

```jsx
const listeners = new Set();

function navigate(url) {
  history.pushState(null, '', url);
  listeners.forEach(notify => notify());
}

function subscribe(notify) {
  listeners.add(notify);
  window.addEventListener('popstate', notify);
  return () => {
    listeners.delete(notify);
    window.removeEventListener('popstate', notify);
  };
}

function useSearchParam(name) {
  return useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () =>
    new URLSearchParams(location.search).get(name)
  );
}

// const post = useSearchParam('post');   navigate('?post=42');
```

The snapshot is a string or `null`, so React can compare it cheaply. If your app uses a router, use its hook instead (e.g. React Router's `useSearchParams`), which already does this.

