Tracking the browser's **location hash** value is the easier of the two tasks. All you need to do is create a custom hook that uses the `useState()` hook to lazily get the `hash` property of the `Location` object.

You can then use the `useCallback()` hook to create a handler that updates the state. Finally, use the `useEffect()` hook to add a listener for the `'hashchange'` event when mounting and clean it up when unmounting.

```jsx
const useHash = () => {
  const [hash, setHash] = React.useState(() => window.location.hash);

  const hashChangeHandler = React.useCallback(() => {
    setHash(window.location.hash);
  }, []);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', hashChangeHandler);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('hashchange', hashChangeHandler);
    };
  }, []);

  const updateHash = React.useCallback(
    newHash => {
      if (newHash !== hash) window.location.hash = newHash;
    },
    [hash]
  );

  return [hash, updateHash];
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const [hash, setHash] = useHash();

  React.useEffect(() => {
    setHash('#list');
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <p>window.location.href: {window.location.href}</p>
      <p>Edit hash: </p>
      <input value={hash} onChange={e => setHash(e.target.value)} />
    </>
  );
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/use-hash-search-param.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Subscribe to the browser's `hashchange` event and read `location.hash` as the current value. The setter just assigns `location.hash`; the browser then fires `hashchange`, and the subscription re-renders the component. The URL stays the single source of truth, so state can't drift from it.

```jsx
function subscribe(notify) {
  window.addEventListener('hashchange', notify);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('hashchange', notify);
}

function useHash() {
  const hash = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => location.hash);
  const setHash = useCallback(next => { location.hash = next; }, []);
  return [hash, setHash];
}

// const [hash, setHash] = useHash();   setHash('#comments');
```

`useSyncExternalStore` is React's built-in way to read an outside value and re-render when it changes, replacing the `useState` + `useEffect` listener pattern. Caveat: every hash assignment adds a browser history entry, and setting the same hash again fires no event.

