Using the `useRef()` hook might have just shifted the problem. The value of the created ref now needs to be **refreshed** inside `setInterval()`. Luckily, this is an easy problem to solve. We could create a wrapper function that passes the function to `setInterval()` instead. This way the function passed to `setInterval()` will never change, but the value of the enclosed ref will always be up to date when it's called.

```jsx
const savedCallback = React.useRef(callback);

React.useEffect(() => {
  function tick() {
    savedCallback.current();
  }
  let id = setInterval(tick, delay);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/use-interval.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
`setInterval(savedCallback.current, delay)` passes the function that is in the ref **at that moment**; later updates to the ref are never seen, so the interval keeps calling the first callback. The wrapper reads the ref **on every tick**, so it always calls the newest callback while the interval itself never has to restart.

```jsx
const savedCallback = useRef(callback);
useEffect(() => {
  savedCallback.current = callback;       // keep the ref fresh
});

useEffect(() => {
  const tick = () => savedCallback.current();
  const id = setInterval(tick, delay);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, [delay]);
```

This "latest ref" pattern is what React 19.2's `useEffectEvent` formalizes: a function the effect can call that always sees current props and state, without being a dependency.

