Tracking the **browser window's dimensions** can be useful for responsive design. The `useWindowSize` hook can be used to track the dimensions of the browser window.

Using the `useState()` hook, you can initialize a state variable that will hold the window's dimensions. Initialize with both values set to `undefined` to avoid mismatch between server and client renders.

Then, create a function that uses `Window.innerWidth` and `Window.innerHeight` to update the state variable. Finally, use the `useEffect()` hook to set an appropriate listener for the `'resize'` event on mount and clean it up when unmounting.

```jsx
const useWindowSize = () => {
  const [windowSize, setWindowSize] = React.useState({
    width: undefined,
    height: undefined,
  });

  React.useEffect(() => {
    const handleResize = () =>
      setWindowSize({ width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight });

    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);

    handleResize();

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    };
  }, []);

  return windowSize;
};

const MyApp = () => {
  const { width, height } = useWindowSize();

  return (
    <p>
      Window size: ({width} x {height})
    </p>
  );
};

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <MyApp />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/window-global-environment-ssr-hooks.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
On the server there's no `window`, and the first client render must produce the **same HTML** as the server for hydration to match. So the initial state is a placeholder, and the effect, which runs only in the browser after hydration, reads `innerWidth`/`innerHeight` and subscribes to `resize`.

```jsx
function useWindowSize() {
  const [size, setSize] = useState({ width: undefined, height: undefined });

  useEffect(() => {
    const update = () => setSize({ width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight });
    update();                                   // read the real size once
    window.addEventListener('resize', update);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', update);
  }, []);

  return size;
}
```

For layout (hide a sidebar, stack columns) prefer CSS media or container queries: no JavaScript, no re-renders, and no flash on first paint. Use the hook when logic needs the number, e.g. choosing how many chart points to render. In a client-only app you could initialize from `window` directly.

