The `useUnload` hook is used to handle the `beforeunload` window event. This event is triggered when the user is **about to close the window**.

In order to implement this hook, you'll need to use the `useRef()` and `useEffect()` hooks. The `useRef()` hook is used to create a ref for the callback function, `fn`. The `useEffect()` hook is used to handle the `'beforeunload'` event by using `EventTarget.addEventListener()`. Finally, `EventTarget.removeEventListener()` is used to perform cleanup after the component is unmounted.

```jsx
const useUnload = fn => {
  const cb = React.useRef(fn);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    const onUnload = cb.current;
    window.addEventListener('beforeunload', onUnload);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', onUnload);
    };
  }, [cb]);
};

const App = () => {
  useUnload(e => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const exit = confirm('Are you sure you want to leave?');
    if (exit) window.close();
  });
  return <div>Try closing the window.</div>;
};
ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <App />
);
```

---
Adapted from [30 seconds of code](https://github.com/30-seconds/30-seconds-of-code/blob/f1b2d5a65c32432a877de94a749be40a396ebed2/content/snippets/react/s/window-global-environment-ssr-hooks.md) · [CC BY 4.0](/licenses/30-seconds.txt).

back:
Add a `beforeunload` listener **only while the form has unsaved changes**, and call `e.preventDefault()` in it. The browser then shows its own generic "Leave site?" dialog. Browsers ignore custom messages and block `alert`/`confirm` during unload, so you can't replace or customize that prompt.

```jsx
function useUnsavedChangesWarning(isDirty) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isDirty) return;
    const handler = e => {
      e.preventDefault();
      e.returnValue = true;    // legacy: Chrome/Edge < 119
    };
    window.addEventListener('beforeunload', handler);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', handler);
  }, [isDirty]);
}

// const isDirty = title !== savedTitle;  useUnsavedChangesWarning(isDirty);
```

Registering it only when dirty matters: a `beforeunload` listener can stop the browser's back/forward cache from working. It also doesn't catch in-app route changes in a single-page app; use your router's navigation blocker for those.

